\subsection{Verifying the improved string representation}

Now let's verify this improved [[__str__]] method works as intended.
The original [[User.__str__]] method returns something like \enquote{User 12345}.
Our improvement returns \enquote{Alice Anderson <alice>}---much more useful for debugging and logging.

<<test functions>>=
class TestUserStringRepresentation:
    """Test improved User.__str__ method"""

    def test_user_str_with_name_and_login(self):
        """User string should show name and login ID"""
        user = User(MagicMock(), {"id": 1, "name": "Alice Anderson",
                                  "login_id": "alice"})

        result = str(user)

        assert result == "Alice Anderson <alice>"

    def test_user_str_without_login_id(self):
        """If login_id is missing, show empty brackets"""
        # Canvas omits login_id, e.g. for users no longer enrolled
        user = User(MagicMock(), {"id": 2, "name": "Bob Brown"})

        result = str(user)

        assert result == "Bob Brown <>"

    def test_user_str_useful_for_debugging(self):
        """String representation should be human-readable"""
        user = User(MagicMock(), {"id": 3, "name": "Charlie Chen",
                                  "login_id": "cchen"})

        # Should not contain Canvas ID or object address
        result = str(user)

        assert "Charlie Chen" in result
        assert "cchen" in result
        assert "0x" not in result  # No memory address
@

This improved string representation makes debugging much easier.
When you print a list of users or log user actions, you see meaningful names instead of numeric IDs.


\section{Keep an edited submission's attachments as [[File]] objects}
\label{SubmissionEditAttachments}

[[canvasapi]] gives a submission two ways of receiving Canvas's JSON, and they
do not agree on the shape of [[attachments]].
The constructor turns each attachment into a [[canvasapi.file.File]], which is
what the rest of canvaslms relies on: [[submissions view]] reads its
[[filename]], looks it up in the attachment cache by [[id]] and calls
[[download]].
[[Submission.edit]], which [[submissions grade]] uses to set grades, comments
and rubric assessments, instead merges the response into the object it was
called on through the generic [[set_attributes]].
That bypasses the constructor, so after an edit [[attachments]] holds the bare
dictionaries from the response:
\begin{minted}{python}
fetched.attachments  # [File(...), ...]
edited.attachments   # [{"id": ..., "filename": ..., ...}, ...]
\end{minted}

In a run that ends right after the edit nobody reads the attachments again, so
the difference was invisible until the submission cache made the edited object
outlive the run.
The cache's lazy wrapper hands [[edit]] straight to the submission it wraps
(\enquote{Writes that Canvas answers} in \cref{sec:lazy-submission-class}),
so the edit rewrites the very object the cache saves on exit.
A submission just graded with a final grade such as P is then never refetched
(the [[NOREFRESH_GRADES]] policy), and every later [[submissions view]] of it
crashed on [[attachment.filename]] (issue~\#503), while [[--no-cache]] and
submissions graded in SpeedGrader worked.

We repair the shape where it is lost: we wrap [[Submission.edit]] so that, once
Canvas's response has been merged, any attachment still in dictionary form is
built into a [[File]] the way the constructor would have built it, with the
submission's own requester.
The check on the type makes this safe whatever Canvas sent: a response without
[[attachments]] leaves the existing [[File]] objects alone, and wrapping twice
changes nothing.
Two alternatives were rejected.
Teaching the readers to accept dictionaries would mean doing it in every one of
them---the formatting for Markdown and JSON, the diff text extraction, the
attachment cache---to restore an invariant that one place can keep.
Invalidating the cache entry after a write (dropping it, or refetching the
submission) would cost an extra request for every submission graded, would need
a way for the CLI to reach into the cache that it does not have, and would
still leave the edited object malformed for the rest of the run, which matters
to interactive grading and to runs without the cache.
Patching the class fixes the cached and the uncached path alike.

The disagreement is a bug in [[canvasapi]] itself, not a convention we must
live with: the constructor's conversion was added without updating the
methods that refresh the object.
We reported it upstream
(\url{https://github.com/ucfopen/canvasapi/issues/721}) and proposed a fix
(\url{https://github.com/ucfopen/canvasapi/pull/722}) that moves the
conversion into [[Submission.set_attributes]], which the constructor already
calls, and lets [[edit]] refresh through it, so that creation and every
refresh share one conversion.
Our patch stays until the minimum [[canvasapi]] version in [[pyproject.toml]]
contains that fix.
It is harmless on a fixed [[canvasapi]]: the attachments then arrive as
[[File]] objects, the type check finds no dictionaries, and the patch changes
nothing.
So raising the minimum version is the only step needed before this section
and its tests can be removed.
<<functions>>=
def make_submission_edit_keep_attachment_files():
  """Make `Submission.edit` keep `attachments` as `File` objects"""
  import canvasapi.file
  import canvasapi.submission

  original_edit = canvasapi.submission.Submission.edit

  @functools.wraps(original_edit)
  def edit(self, **kwargs):
    result = original_edit(self, **kwargs)
    <<turn dictionaries in [[self.attachments]] into [[File]] objects>>
    return result

  canvasapi.submission.Submission.edit = edit
@

The conversion mirrors the one in the [[Submission]] constructor.
<<turn dictionaries in [[self.attachments]] into [[File]] objects>>=
self.attachments = [
  canvasapi.file.File(self._requester, attachment)
  if isinstance(attachment, dict) else attachment
  for attachment in self.attachments
]
@

\subsection{Verifying that edits keep [[File]] attachments}

Let us verify the patch on a real [[Submission]] built from a dictionary, with
a stand-in requester that answers the edit with the same attachment in JSON
form, as Canvas does.
Afterwards the attachment must still be a [[File]] carrying the name.
A response without [[attachments]] must leave the existing [[File]] alone.
<<test functions>>=
class TestSubmissionEditKeepsAttachmentFiles:
    """Submission.edit leaves attachments as File objects (issue #503)."""

    ATTACHMENT = {"id": 7, "filename": "report.pdf",
                  "display_name": "report.pdf",
                  "content-type": "application/pdf"}

    def make_submission(self, response):
        """A submission with one attachment whose edit answers `response`."""
        requester = MagicMock()
        requester.request.return_value.json.return_value = response
        return Submission(requester, {
            "id": 3, "assignment_id": 2, "user_id": 1, "course_id": 4,
            "grade": None, "attachments": [dict(self.ATTACHMENT)]})

    def test_edit_rebuilds_attachment_files(self):
        """The attachments in the edit response become File objects."""
        submission = self.make_submission({
            "id": 3, "assignment_id": 2, "user_id": 1, "grade": "P",
            "attachments": [dict(self.ATTACHMENT)]})

        assert submission.edit(submission={"posted_grade": "P"}) is submission

        [attachment] = submission.attachments
        assert isinstance(attachment, File)
        assert attachment.filename == "report.pdf"

    def test_response_without_attachments_keeps_files(self):
        """A response that omits attachments leaves the File objects."""
        submission = self.make_submission({
            "id": 3, "assignment_id": 2, "user_id": 1, "grade": "P"})
        [before] = submission.attachments

        submission.edit(submission={"posted_grade": "P"})

        assert submission.attachments == [before]
        assert isinstance(before, File)
@


\section{Cacheable Canvas objects}

We would like certain methods in certain Canvas objects to be cached.
Particularly, methods that return objects that rarely change should be 
cacheable.
For instance, [[canvas.get_courses()]] returns a list of courses.
This list changes rarely\footnote{%
  This happens four times per year, when IT creates the new Canvas rooms 270 
  days ahead of course start.
  It also happens when someone adds you to manually to a course.
}, so its results can and should be cached.
The list of students changes usually only once in the beginning of the course.

Results on the other hand changes during a course.
However, once a student receives a pass, the result will (usually) not change 
again.
There are these cases however, where a student complements their submission to 
get a higher grade.
But once the student has the highest grade, an A or a P, the result will not 
change.

We will use the [[cachetools]] package to provide suitable caching decorators 
for the [[canvasapi]] classes' methods.
However, in some cases we can't, but need to write our own caching mechanisms.
But this means that we can construct quite complex cache policies.

The general design is this:
We add a cache attribute, then the methods store objects (return values) there.
The [[Canvas]] object stores [[Course]] objects.
Each [[Course]] object stores [[Assignment]] objects.
Each [[Assignment]] object stores [[Submission]] objects.
This means we can store and restore the entire hierarchy just by storing the 
[[Canvas]] object.
If we pickle the [[Canvas]] object, the caches of the [[Course]] objects will 
be in there, the [[Assignment]] caches will in turn be in them, and so on.

When we want to force an update of the courses, we don't want to clear the 
cache.
Then we lose the entire hierarchy.
We can update the list instead of clearing the entire cache hierarchy.
That way we can update objects and keep their caches.
But we'll need a way to know when to update.

\subsection{Syncing caches}

We usually have two methods to cache:
\begin{center}
[[get_x(id, /, **kwargs)]]
\quad
and
\quad
[[get_xs(**kwargs)]].
\end{center}
For example,
\begin{center}
[[get_submission(id, /, **kwargs)]]
\quad
and
\quad
[[get_submissions(**kwargs)]].
\end{center}
These should sync.
In Canvas we can also pass options through keyword arguments, a common one 
being [[include]] which specifies a list of things to include.
For instance, when it comes to submissions we can ask Canvas to include grading 
rubrics.
This means that we want to maintain these details, so that we don't lose 
information in the cache.

This means that we need two functions to support the caching:
\begin{description}
\item [[must_update(prev_kwargs, kwargs)]] takes the old keyword 
arguments ([[prev_kwargs]]) to check if they're a superset of the new keyword 
arguments ([[kwargs]]).
If not, the cache doesn't have all necessary information and we must fetch it,
with the largest set of what has been asked for so far.
\item [[merge_kwargs]] will take a list of keyword arguments dictionaries and 
merge them.
Most of the time these contain only the key [[include]] whose value is a list 
of things to include.
So essentially, we want to take the union of all these sets.
\end{description}
Superset means that every item asked for is among those the cache has.
It's not enough that the new list has more items than the old one.
A roster cached with [[["enrollments", "avatar_url"]]] has no email addresses,
although [[["email", "enrollments"]]] is not a superset of it either, and each
list has something the other lacks.
Since the fetch asks for the union, the next request for either list finds
everything in the cache, so two callers wanting different things don't take
turns fetching.

Canvas accepts a single value as well as a list, so we count a string as a list
of one item.
What counts as several values is for [[canvasapi]] to say, since it is what
builds the request: its [[is_multivalued]] takes any iterable except a string or
bytes as several values, a tuple, a set or a generator as much as a list.
We read values the same way, with [[as_list]] below, so that what we compare
and record is what [[canvasapi]] sends.
Taking only a list as several would make a tuple of includes one include, the
tuple itself, which is not what Canvas gets.
The merge stores the items as a list, which matters beyond this code: the cache
file is shared with older copies of the library, which only know lists.

A generator is multivalued too, but it can be read only once.
Comparing it with what is cached would use it up, and the request would then
go out without the includes, or the cache would store an exhausted generator,
which can't even be pickled.
So the cached methods read such an argument into a list as they are called,
before anything else looks at it.
Collections that can be read again, such as tuples and sets, are passed on as
they are.
<<kwargs functions caches>>=
def with_iterators_read(kwargs):
  """Return `kwargs` with each one-shot iterator, such as a generator, as a list."""
  return {key: list(value) if isinstance(value, collections.abc.Iterator)
               else value
          for key, value in kwargs.items()}
@

We can thus start with the following defaults:
<<kwargs functions caches>>=
def as_list(value):
  """Returns the values in `value` as a list, the way canvasapi reads them.

  An iterable other than a string or bytes is several values; anything else,
  `None` included, is a list of one.
  """
  return list(value) if canvasapi.util.is_multivalued(value) else [value]

def must_update(prev_kwargs, new_kwargs,
                ignore_keys=[
                  <<keys to ignore>>
                ]):
  """
  Returns True if we must update the cache (refetch).

  <<doc for keys to ignore>>
  """
  for key, value in new_kwargs.items():
    # Skip ignored keys (like sorting parameters)
    if key in ignore_keys:
      continue

    if key not in prev_kwargs:
      return True
    elif is_multivalued(value) or is_multivalued(prev_kwargs[key]):
      if not set(as_list(value)) <= set(as_list(prev_kwargs[key])):
        return True
    elif value != prev_kwargs[key]:
      return True

  return False

def merge_kwargs(kwargs_list,
                 ignore_keys=[
                   <<keys to ignore>>
                 ]):
  """
  Merges a list of keyword arguments dictionaries. Lists are unioned, and so are
  values of `include` given as strings. All other non-list keys (usually strings)
  must be the same in all dictionaries.

  <<doc for keys to ignore>>
  """
  new_kwargs = dict()

  for kwargs in kwargs_list:
    for key, value in kwargs.items():
      if key not in new_kwargs:
        new_kwargs[key] = list(value) if is_multivalued(value) else value
      elif (is_multivalued(value) or is_multivalued(new_kwargs[key])
            or key == "include"):
        new_kwargs[key] = list(set(as_list(new_kwargs[key]))
                               | set(as_list(value)))
      else:
        <<if [[key]] can be ignored>>:
          new_kwargs[key] = value
        elif value != new_kwargs[key]:
          raise ValueError(f"Cannot merge {key} with "
                           f"{value} and {new_kwargs[key]}")

  return new_kwargs
@

