\subsubsection{Objects that were stored before their class was decorated}
\label{sec:cache-attributes-on-read}

The constructor is not the only way an object comes to be.
One that is read from the cache file is unpickled, and unpickling sets the
attributes that were stored without running any constructor.
An object stored before its class cached this attribute, by an earlier version
or by another program with an older copy of this library, therefore has no
[[_cache]] and no [[_all_fetched]], and the wrapped methods would fail on
their first line.

That used to be why caching one more attribute needed a new schema version in
[[cli/cache.nw]], which discards everyone's cache file, and with programs of
different versions sharing the file, discards it again and again.
It is simpler to make the attributes when they are first needed.
The wrapped methods run the constructor's chunk before anything else, so an
object without the attributes gets them empty, which is the truth: nothing of
this kind has been fetched through it.
In the other direction nothing is needed, since a copy that does not cache the
attribute never looks at it.

Code outside the wrapped methods that touches a cache, to add what a command
has just created, say, cannot assume the attributes either.
It gets the cache through [[cached_entries]].
<<functions>>=
def cached_entries(owner, attr_name):
  """Return the cache dictionary of `attr_name` on `owner`.

  Creates the cache attributes if `owner` was stored before its class cached
  `attr_name`.
  """
  self = owner
  <<construct [[attr_name]] cache attributes in [[self]]>>
  return getattr(owner, f"{attr_name}_cache")
@

Let's verify that an object without the attributes works through both
methods, and that it fetches rather than answering from a cache it never had.

<<test functions>>=
class TestCacheAttributesOnRead:
    """Test an object stored before its class cached the attribute"""

    def stored_before(self):
        """Return an instance as unpickling an older file would give it."""
        class TestClass:
            def __init__(self):
                self.requests = 0

            def get_items(self, **kwargs):
                self.requests += 1
                return [canvas_object(id=1)]

            def get_item(self, item_id, **kwargs):
                self.requests += 1
                return canvas_object(id=item_id)

        CacheGetMethods("item")(TestClass)
        instance = TestClass()
        del instance.item_cache, instance.item_all_fetched
        return instance

    def test_the_listing_is_fetched_and_then_cached(self):
        instance = self.stored_before()

        assert [item.id for item in instance.get_items()] == [1]
        assert [item.id for item in instance.get_items()] == [1]
        assert instance.requests == 1

    def test_one_object_is_fetched_and_then_cached(self):
        instance = self.stored_before()

        assert instance.get_item(2).id == 2
        assert instance.get_item(2).id == 2
        assert instance.requests == 1

    def test_other_code_gets_the_cache_too(self):
        instance = self.stored_before()

        entries = cached_entries(instance, "item")

        assert entries == {} and entries is instance.item_cache
        assert instance.item_all_fetched is None
