\subsection{Copy cache to new object}
\label{sec:cache-copy-to-new}

When we update all objects, we want to keep the caches of every old object.
Otherwise, whenever we update all objects we lose all caches further down the 
hierarchy.
When we construct a new object, we want to copy the cache from the old object.

We can do this by finding all cache-related attributes and copying them.
The problem is that we don't know what attributes an object has caches for.
So we'll do some pattern matching on the attribute names.
We'll look for attributes that end with [[_cache]] and [[_all_fetched]], and
with [[_all_kwargs]], the record of what a listing has been asked for
(\cref{sec:what-a-listing-asks-for}).

Note that we use [[cache_attr_name]] as the loop variable, not [[attr_name]].
This is critical to avoid shadowing the closure variable [[attr_name]] that was
captured in line~371.
If we used [[attr_name]] as the loop variable, Python would treat it as a local
variable throughout the entire [[new_get_attrs]] function, causing an
[[UnboundLocalError]] when we try to access it before the loop (in
lines~494--495).
<<update [[obj]] with cache from [[old_obj]]>>=
for cache_attr_name in dir(old_obj):
  if cache_attr_name.endswith(("_cache", "_all_fetched", "_all_kwargs",
                               "__unlisted")):
    setattr(obj, cache_attr_name, getattr(old_obj, cache_attr_name))
@

The same reasoning holds for every other way an object gets replaced, not only
the bulk fetch: the singular method and the individual refresh of a listing also
build a new object from Canvas's answer.
Those two used to store the new object as it came, which silently threw away
everything cached below it.
A single [[canvas.get_course(id, include=["syllabus_body"])]] from the
[[syllabus]] commands thus forgot that course's assignments, users and
submissions, though nothing about them had changed.
All three paths therefore share this chunk.

A re-fetch must not lose the details of the object itself either.
The cache entry remembers which keyword arguments produced it, and
[[must_update]] trusts that record: after a plain [[get_item(1)]] has refreshed
an object fetched with [[include=["a"]]], a later request for [[a]] must not
find it missing.
The singular method therefore fetches with the union of the old and new keyword
arguments, as the bulk fetch already does.

Let's verify both, for each way of replacing an object.
The stand-in class is enough here: what we test is what the decorator does with
the objects it receives, and a recording could not express Canvas's answer
changing between the calls.

<<test functions>>=
class TestRefetchKeepsCachedDetails:
    """Test that replacing an object loses nothing cached about it"""

    def make_instance(self, fetches=None):
        """Return a decorated instance with item 1 listed and given children"""
        class TestClass:
            def get_items(self, **kwargs):
                return [canvas_object(Submission, id=1, grade="F")]
            def get_item(self, item_id, **kwargs):
                if fetches is not None:
                    fetches.append(kwargs)
                return canvas_object(Submission, id=item_id, grade="F")

        CacheGetMethods("item")(TestClass)
        instance = TestClass()
        list(instance.get_items())
        obj, _ = instance.item_cache[1]
        obj.child_cache = {"sentinel": "subtree"}
        obj.child_all_fetched = datetime(2026, 1, 1)
        obj.child_all_kwargs = {"include": ["asked of the child listing"]}
        return instance

    def age(self, obj):
        """Make obj stale by the submission TTL"""
        obj._fetched_at = datetime.now() - timedelta(
            minutes=SUBMISSION_TTL_MINUTES + 1)

    def assert_children_kept(self, obj):
        assert obj.child_cache == {"sentinel": "subtree"}
        assert obj.child_all_fetched == datetime(2026, 1, 1)
        assert obj.child_all_kwargs == {
            "include": ["asked of the child listing"]}

    def test_singular_refetch_for_new_kwargs_keeps_children(self):
        instance = self.make_instance()
        self.assert_children_kept(instance.get_item(1, include=["more"]))

    def test_singular_refetch_of_stale_object_keeps_children(self):
        instance = self.make_instance()
        self.age(instance.item_cache[1][0])
        self.assert_children_kept(instance.get_item(1))

    def test_individual_refresh_in_listing_keeps_children(self):
        instance = self.make_instance()
        self.age(instance.item_cache[1][0])
        self.assert_children_kept(list(instance.get_items())[0])

    def test_bulk_refetch_keeps_children(self):
        instance = self.make_instance()
        old, _ = instance.item_cache[1]
        instance.item_all_fetched = None
        new = list(instance.get_items())[0]
        assert new is not old
        self.assert_children_kept(new)

    def test_stale_refetch_keeps_earlier_includes(self):
        fetches = []
        instance = self.make_instance(fetches)
        instance.get_item(1, include=["a"])
        self.age(instance.item_cache[1][0])
        instance.get_item(1)
        assert fetches[-1] == {"include": ["a"]}
        assert instance.item_cache[1][1] == {"include": ["a"]}
