\subsection{What a listing asks for}
\label{sec:what-a-listing-asks-for}

A refetch of a listing asks for the union of what was asked before, so that it
loses nothing.
The question is whose requests count.
Until issue~\#585 it was every entry's: each entry records the keyword arguments
it was fetched with, and the listing took the union of those records.
That mixes two kinds of request.
A request of the listing asks something about every object in it, and the next
listing must ask the same.
A request of one object asks something about that object only.
The [[syllabus]] command makes such a request,
[[get_course(id, include=["syllabus_body"])]], and through the union of the
records it became a request of the listing: the next course listing asked for
the syllabus of every course, and so did every later one, as the union never
shrinks.
Syllabi can be large, the course listing holds every course the user has ever
had, and since issue~\#579 it is fetched daily.

So the listing keeps a record of its own, [[{attr}_all_kwargs]] on the owner,
next to [[{attr}_all_fetched]]: the union of what has been asked of the
listing, and of nothing else.
A singular request leaves it alone.

A cache written by an earlier version has no such record, and neither has an
owner that an older copy of this library replaced without keeping it, see below.
The union of the entries is then the best we know, so we fall back on it once;
the fetch records it, and from then on the listing has a record.
That fallback can't tell which of those includes came from one object, so a
cache that has already learnt to ask for the syllabi of all courses goes on doing
so until [[canvaslms cache clear]].
This needs no bump of [[CACHE_SCHEMA_VERSION]]: the record is read with a default,
and older copies of the library do not read it at all.
<<let [[earlier_kwargs]] be what was asked of the listing before>>=
listing_kwargs = getattr(self, f"{attr_name}_all_kwargs", None)
if listing_kwargs is None:
  earlier_kwargs = [without_restrictions(entry_kwargs)
                    for _, entry_kwargs in attr_cache.values()]
else:
  earlier_kwargs = [listing_kwargs]
@

The record belongs to the owner, and the owner is itself an entry of another
listing, a course in the course listing for instance.
When that listing replaces the course, the record must go with it, as its child
listings do, or each daily refetch of the courses would make the next fetch of
each roster fall back on the union again.
That is why its name ends in [[_all_kwargs]]: the attributes copied to a new
object are recognized by their endings (\cref{sec:cache-copy-to-new}).

Keeping a singular request out of the listing has a consequence for the entry
that was fetched with it.
The listing replaces it with an object that lacks what was asked for, the
syllabus in our example.
If we stored that object with the listing's arguments, the next
[[get_course(id, include=["syllabus_body"])]] would fetch the course again, as it
should, since the entry does not have it, and so on after every listing.
We would have traded a large listing for a singular request after each one.
So when an entry has been fetched with more than the listing asks for, the new
object takes over the attributes that only the old one has, and the entry keeps
the record of what it was fetched with, merged with the listing's.
The entry then answers its own singular request from the cache, as it did before
the listing.

We copy the attributes the new object lacks, rather than the ones the includes
name, because an include does not always name the attribute it adds:
[[include=["course_image"]]] gives [[image_download_url]], for instance.
Names starting with an underscore are ours, not Canvas's: [[_fetched_at]] is set
anew and [[_unlisted]] must not survive a listing that returned the object.
The cached child listings are copied by the chunk before this one.
One kind of attribute only looks missing.
For every field whose value reads as a date, [[canvasapi]] adds a parsed copy,
[[end_at_date]] for [[end_at]], and when Canvas has since cleared the date, the
new object has [[end_at]] as [[None]] and no [[end_at_date]].
Copying the old one back would contradict the field it was parsed from, so a
name ending in [[_date]] whose field the new object has is left out.

The price is freshness.
Data that only a singular request asked for is as old as that request; the
listing no longer refreshes it.
A course's syllabus therefore stays as it was cached until
[[--refresh-cache]], or until something else makes the singular method fetch the
course again.
That is how a single object's data behaved before the course listing expired at
all, and it is what [[--refresh-cache]] is for.
<<keep what [[old_obj]] was fetched with beyond the listing>>=
if must_update(union_kwargs, without_restrictions(old_kwargs)):
  for name, value in vars(old_obj).items():
    if (not name.startswith("_") and name not in vars(obj)
        and not (name.endswith("_date") and name[:-5] in vars(obj))):
      setattr(obj, name, value)
  <<carry over the mark of short enrollments>>
  obj_kwargs = merge_kwargs([union_kwargs, without_restrictions(old_kwargs)])
