Several of the tuning constants below can be overridden through
environment variables.
A bare [[int()]] or [[float()]] of the environment value would let a
malformed variable---say [[CANVASLMS_SUBMISSION_TTL_MINUTES=off]]---raise
[[ValueError]] at import time, aborting \emph{every} subcommand (even
[[--help]]) with a raw traceback.
A tuning knob must not be able to brick the CLI, so we read these values
through a helper that falls back to the default with a warning instead.
The helper tangles into the module before the constants (see the module
outline), since the constants call it at import time.

\subsection{When to report a malformed setting}
\label{sec:setting-problems}

The warning has two audiences with opposing needs.
A command-line user must be able to silence it with [[-q]], and this is
decisive for [[ungraded.sh]], which runs from cron: cron mails everything on
stderr, so a single malformed setting would send mail on every run and
[[-q]] would be no remedy.
A library caller, on the other hand, never runs [[main]] and still deserves
to hear about the typo.

Logging the warning at import, where the constants are computed, serves
neither.
[[main]] configures logging only after it has parsed the command line,
which is after this module was imported, so [[-q]] has no say yet and Python's
handler of last resort prints the message bare, without the [[WARNING]] tag
that the other messages carry.

We considered the opposite design, in which the settings are read when they
are used, so that logging has been configured by then.
We rejected it because these settings are module constants with many use sites
(the cache help, [[outdated]], [[retry_after_miss]], the bulk refresh), and the
tests replace them with [[monkeypatch.setattr]].
Turning them into function calls would touch all of that for no other gain.

So [[env_number]] only \emph{records} the problem, keyed by variable name
(a variable is therefore reported at most once), and a second function reports
what has been recorded.
It is called from the two places where logging is known to be settled:
[[main]], right after it has configured logging, and the wrappers that
[[CacheGetMethods]] puts around every cached [[get_*]] method.
The latter is the first use of the cache by a library caller; for a command
line the report has long been made by then, and finding nothing to report is a
single test of an empty dictionary.
A command that exits during argument parsing, such as [[--help]], comes before
either and reports nothing; the help shows the default that is in effect.
<<environment helpers>>=
SETTING_PROBLEMS = {}

def env_number(name, default, parse):
  """Read the numeric setting `name` from the environment.

  Converts with `parse` (int or float); falls back to `default` if the
  variable is set but doesn't parse. The problem is not logged here but
  recorded for `report_setting_problems`, since logging may not be
  configured yet when this runs at import.
  """
  value = os.environ.get(name)
  if value is None:
    return default
  try:
    return parse(value)
  except ValueError:
    SETTING_PROBLEMS[name] = (f"ignoring {name}={value!r}: "
                              f"not a number, using default {default}")
    return default

def report_setting_problems():
  """Log each recorded malformed setting once, as a warning."""
  while SETTING_PROBLEMS:
    _, message = SETTING_PROBLEMS.popitem()
    logger.warning(message)
@

