\subsection{Verifying the reports of a malformed setting}

The tests above show the mechanism; what matters is that the promised outcomes
hold from the outside.
We run the real entry point in a subprocess, as cron does, because the order
of import, logging configuration and warning is exactly what is under test and
cannot be reproduced within pytest's own process.
The command [[cache clear-attachments]] needs no network and no credentials,
and it runs past argument parsing; the home directories point into
[[tmp_path]] so that it cannot touch the user's own caches.
We set a malformed setting and compare three runs.

<<test functions>>=
def run_in_subprocess(tmp_path, code, *args):
  """Run `code` with a malformed setting; return the completed process."""
  import subprocess
  env = dict(os.environ)
  env.update(HOME=str(tmp_path), XDG_CONFIG_HOME=str(tmp_path / "c"),
             XDG_CACHE_HOME=str(tmp_path / "k"),
             XDG_DATA_HOME=str(tmp_path / "d"),
             PYTHONPATH=os.pathsep.join(sys.path),
             CANVASLMS_COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS="abc")
  return subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-c", code, *args], env=env,
                        capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)


CLI_ENTRY = "import canvaslms.cli; canvaslms.cli.main()"
CLEAR_ATTACHMENTS = ["cache", "clear-attachments", "--older-than-days", "9999"]


def test_cli_warns_once_about_malformed_setting(tmp_path):
  result = run_in_subprocess(tmp_path, CLI_ENTRY, *CLEAR_ATTACHMENTS)
  assert result.returncode == 0
  lines = [line for line in result.stderr.splitlines()
           if "CANVASLMS_COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS" in line]
  assert len(lines) == 1
  assert lines[0].startswith("[WARNING] ")


def test_cli_quiet_hides_malformed_setting_warning(tmp_path):
  result = run_in_subprocess(tmp_path, CLI_ENTRY, "-q", *CLEAR_ATTACHMENTS)
  assert result.returncode == 0
  assert result.stderr == ""


