The list of courses rarely changes, but when it does the cost of not noticing
is unusually high.
A pattern like [[-c prgi]] that still matches last year's course raises no
error when this year's is missing from the cache: the command silently works on
the wrong course.
The refresh, on the other hand, is cheap, since it re-reads only the listing and
every course keeps its cached contents (\cref{sec:cache-copy-to-new}).
That imbalance is why [[COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS]] is the shortest of the listing
intervals, one day by default.

This one cannot work like the others, for two reasons.
The others hang off [[outdated]], which the listing methods call for each object
\emph{in} their cache, so that it can expire that object's child listings.
The [[Canvas]] object is the root of the hierarchy and therefore in no cache, so
nothing would ever ask whether its listing had expired.

\label{sec:course-listing-outage}
The other reason is that nearly every command starts by reading this listing.
Resetting the timestamp, as the others do, makes the next read fetch or fail, so
one day after the last fetch every command would crash if Canvas could not be
reached, although the cache holds what most of them need.
The refresh is a precaution, not something the command asked for, so its failure
must not cost the command its answer.
The method itself therefore checks the age, as it reads the listing, and falls
back on what it has.
That only holds when the cached listing would otherwise have been good enough, so
this comes after the checks for what cannot be served from the cache: a
restriction left by an older version, a requested refresh or new keyword
arguments that an entry Canvas still lists lacks.
Those still fail loudly, since the cached listing is not what the caller asked for.
An entry Canvas no longer lists is different: the fetch it causes is a precaution
too (\cref{sec:subset-listings}), so it takes the same path as this one.
<<see whether the course listing has expired>>=
course_listing_expired = (
  attr_name == "course" and attr_all_fetched
  and datetime.now() - attr_all_fetched > timedelta(days=COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS))
if course_listing_expired:
  logger.info(f"Cache invalidation: course_all_fetched periodic refresh "
              f"(fetched: {attr_all_fetched}, "
              f"threshold: {COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS} days)")
  precaution = True
@

Falling back is not enough if it comes late.
The retry policy of [[configure_canvas_retry_policy]] is made for requests a
command depends on: it tries a failed connection ten more times, and its waits add
up to about 17 minutes.
A command that could have answered from the cache would spend them waiting for
something it does not need.
So a precaution gets one try, through [[without_retries]].
The price is that a passing failure, which the retries would have overcome, skips
this refresh; the next try comes soon enough.
We catch the errors of an outage, the [[CanvasException]]s of an answer and the
[[RequestException]]s of no answer, and nothing else, so that a bug in our own
code still shows.
<<refresh as a precaution, or keep the cached listing>>=
try:
  with without_retries([self]):
    <<fetch all and populate [[attr_cache]]>>
except (canvasapi.exceptions.CanvasException,
        requests.exceptions.RequestException) as error:
  logger.warning(f"Could not refresh {display_plural}, "
                 f"using the cached ones: {error}")
  <<put off the next precaution>>
else:
  <<yield all objects in [[attr_cache]]>>
  return
@

A host that does not answer at all still takes until the connection times out,
and a command can read the listing more than once.
So a failure also puts off the next try.
For the course listing it is put off by [[MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES]], the
interval we already use for how soon looking again is worth it.
Changing the timestamp does this without new state in the shared cache file, and
older copies of this library do not expire this listing at all, so its value
means nothing to them.

That trick does not work for the other precaution, the one for entries Canvas no
longer lists.
It is due once the listing is [[MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES]] old, so putting it
off by as much would mean dating the listing to now, and the listing's own
time to live would then count from a fetch that failed.
So we remember the failure instead, for the rest of the run, in
[[failed_precautions]]: per kind of listing, the owners whose precaution failed.
A later read in the same run then serves the cache without a word, and so the
user is warned once.
The next run tries again, once, which is what a run started after Canvas came
back needs.
The set is weak, so it keeps no owner alive and an owner that is gone can't be
mistaken for a new one; it is not pickled, so it ends with the run.
A failed course refresh is remembered there as well, or the read after it would
try the listing again for an entry that left.
<<put off the next precaution>>=
failed_precautions[attr_name].add(self)
if course_listing_expired:
  attr_all_fetched = (datetime.now()
                      - timedelta(days=COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS)
                      + timedelta(minutes=MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES))
  setattr(self, f"{attr_name}_all_fetched", attr_all_fetched)
<<functions>>=
failed_precautions = collections.defaultdict(weakref.WeakSet)
@

The retry policy lives in the adapters of the session, which all objects from one
client share, so we swap the adapters for as long as the precaution lasts and put
them back afterwards.
The session is reached differently from the [[Canvas]] object and from the others,
and a stand-in in the tests may have none.
The block must not yield to the caller: whatever the caller did in the meantime
would lose its retries too.
<<functions>>=
@contextlib.contextmanager
def without_retries(owners):
  """Make the requests in this block give up at the first failure.

  `owners` are the Canvas objects whose requests are concerned.
  """
  sessions = {}
  for owner in owners:
    requester = (getattr(owner, "_requester", None)
                 or getattr(owner, "_Canvas__requester", None))
    session = getattr(requester, "_session", None)
    if session is not None:
      sessions[id(session)] = session

  kept_adapters = [(session, session.adapters)
                   for session in sessions.values()]
  try:
    for session, _ in kept_adapters:
      session.adapters = collections.OrderedDict()
      session.mount("https://", requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter(max_retries=0))
      session.mount("http://", requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter(max_retries=0))
    yield
  finally:
    for session, adapters in kept_adapters:
      session.adapters = adapters
@

