\subsection{Requests for a subset of a listing}
\label{sec:subset-listings}

Some query arguments are additive, such as [[include]]: they ask for more about
the same objects, and fit naturally in a cache that merges earlier requests into
later ones.
Others are restrictive, such as [[enrollment_type=["student"]]]: they describe a
subset of the collection, not a richer version of it.

Treating the second kind like the first is not merely imprecise, it corrupts the
listing for everyone else.
Here is how it happened.
The FeedbackFruits tooling asked a course for
[[get_users(enrollment_type=["student"], include=["email", "enrollments"])]].
The cache took the answer for the course's complete roster and stored the
restriction on each entry.
From then on [[users]] and [[nytid]] asked for
[[get_users(include=["enrollments"])]], found that satisfied, and saw no TAs.
A refresh did not help, because it merges the keyword arguments of the entries
into its request, so it asked Canvas for students only, again.
Only [[--no-cache]], with no entries to merge, could find a TA.

The cache cannot serve a subset from a listing, or a listing from a subset,
without knowing what each argument means.
So it does not try.
A request with an argument we do not know to be additive or harmless, as the keys
for sorting and paging are, goes straight to Canvas
and leaves the cache alone, so the caller gets the right answer and nobody else
is affected.
Callers wanting speed can still fetch the full collection and filter locally,
which is what [[canvaslms.cli.users]], [[canvaslms.cli.submissions]] and
[[canvaslms.cli.assignments]] do.

This protects every user of the library, not only the [[canvaslms]] command,
since the check is in the decorated method itself.
The contrast with our other refreshes is deliberate: [[--refresh-cache]] and the
retry on a miss are things a caller must ask for or pass a pattern to, while a
correct listing must not depend on how it is reached.
<<constants>>=
COMPLETE_LISTING_KEYS = (
  "include",
  <<keys to ignore>>
)
<<kwargs functions caches>>=
def restricts_listing(kwargs):
  """Return True if `kwargs` may ask for only a subset of a listing."""
  return any(key not in COMPLETE_LISTING_KEYS for key in kwargs)

def without_restrictions(kwargs):
  """Return `kwargs` with only what applies to a complete listing."""
  return {key: value for key, value in kwargs.items()
          if key in COMPLETE_LISTING_KEYS}
<<pass a request for a subset through to Canvas>>=
if restricts_listing(kwargs):
  logger.info(f"Not cached: {display_plural} restricted by {kwargs}")
  yield from get_attrs(self, *args, **kwargs)
  return
