An entry Canvas did not return can also hold a listing back in another way.
It keeps the includes of the last fetch that returned it, so a student who has
left the course lacks whatever the listing has been asked for since.
Fetching the listing can't give them that while Canvas doesn't list them, so if
the check above counted them like any other entry, every read asking for more
than they have would fetch the whole listing again.
But we can't stop asking either: Canvas may list them again, for instance when
an enrollment is reactivated, and only a fetch can tell us that.

So a fetch marks the entries it did not get back, and when the plural method
compares the keyword arguments, a marked entry only counts once the listing is
old enough, by the same rule as a name that matches nothing
(\cref{sec:refresh-on-miss}).
The reasoning is also the same: we asked Canvas recently and that answer still
holds, so the time of the fetch works as the negative cache and we need no other
state.
Until then, someone who has returned is served without that include.

What this saves is the repeated fetches of a burst of reads, not the fetches
themselves.
Someone who has left for good goes on lacking the include, so a program asking
for it pays for one fetch per [[MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES]], and so for one
per run if its runs are further apart than that (issue~\#588).
A program like [[fbf]], which always asks for email addresses, thus gets no help
from the cache in a course that someone has left.
A single-object fetch that Canvas answers also replaces the entry and with it the
mark, so the next read asking for more can cost one more fetch within the interval.
We accept these costs since the alternative is to serve someone who has returned
without what was asked for.
A cheaper way would be to ask Canvas about the marked entries alone, one request
per person who left and include, with an occasional listing to find those who
return.
That trades one listing for several requests and needs a record of what was
asked of whom, so we have not done it.

Such a fetch is also not something the caller needs: everyone Canvas still lists
has what was asked for, so the cached listing answers the call, and the fetch is
only a precaution in case someone has returned.
So it is treated like the refresh of an expired course listing
(\cref{sec:course-listing-outage}): it gets one try, and if Canvas can't be
reached the cached listing is used, with a warning.
Without this, Canvas being down turned a command that could have answered from
the cache into 17~minutes of retries and a failure.
A failure is remembered for the rest of the run, so later reads don't try again.
An entry Canvas still lists that lacks what is asked for is another matter, and
then the fetch is needed and gets the full retry policy, as before.
What an entry was fetched with is read through [[kept_kwargs]]
(\cref{sec:short-enrollments}), so an entry whose enrollments the cache file
shortened too much for this process lacks them.
<<find out whether the cached entries have what is asked for>>=
lacking = [(cached_obj, prev_kwargs)
           for cached_obj, prev_kwargs in attr_cache.values()
           if must_update(kept_kwargs(cached_obj, prev_kwargs), kwargs)]
listed_lacking = [(cached_obj, prev_kwargs)
                  for cached_obj, prev_kwargs in lacking
                  if not getattr(cached_obj, "_unlisted", False)]
if listed_lacking:
  logger.info(f"Cache invalidation: {display_plural} - new kwargs required "
              f"(existing: {listed_lacking[0][1]}, requested: {kwargs})")
  attr_all_fetched = None
elif (lacking and old_enough_to_ask_again(attr_all_fetched)
      and self not in failed_precautions[attr_name]):
  logger.info(f"Cache invalidation: {display_plural} - {len(lacking)} entries "
              f"Canvas no longer listed lack {kwargs}")
  precaution = True
