Clearing a restriction, as above, only removes a claim about the listing.
Recording the new includes on these entries would add a claim about the object,
and a false one, since it doesn't have that data.
A request for that one object would then be served without it, for instance
[[get_course(id, include=["syllabus_body"])]] for a course that is no longer in
the listing.
With the mark, that request still sees what is missing and asks Canvas, which
gives it or raises an error if it no longer has the object.
What the listing was asked for when they were in it still counts towards the
largest set, as the listing records it (\cref{sec:what-a-listing-asks-for}), and
an entry that Canvas returns again is a new object without the mark.

A cache written by an earlier version has no marks, so it costs one more fetch,
after which it has them.

<<mark entries Canvas did not return>>=
fetched_keys = {getattr(fetched, cache_key) for fetched in fetched_objs}
for obj_key, (cached_obj, _) in attr_cache.items():
  if obj_key not in fetched_keys:
    cached_obj._unlisted = True
