\subsection{Forgetting what Canvas no longer lists}
\label{sec:forget-unlisted}

Marking an entry Canvas did not return keeps it in the cache, and so far that was
the rule for every listing: a fetch added and updated entries but never removed
any.
A course you have left, or an assignment deleted in Canvas, thus stayed, and the
listings went on yielding it, also right after [[--refresh-cache]], which a user
does not expect from a flag with that name (issue~\#589).
Only [[--no-cache]] or [[cache clear]] showed what Canvas has now.
Such entries also add to the cache file that every command loads and saves, and
they lack whatever the listing has been asked for since, which costs the fetches
described above.

Whether to remove them depends on the kind of object.
A student who has left a course still has results that count, and [[results]]
must find them, so users keep their entries, and so do submissions
(\cref{sec:unlisted-submissions}); they are marked, as above.
A course you no longer have access to, or an assignment that has been deleted,
has no such use: commands work on what is in Canvas.
So the decorator takes [[forget_unlisted]], and the course and assignment
listings set it.

Removing is only safe when we know that Canvas did not return the entry, not
merely that we did not see it.
That holds after a complete fetch of the listing.
We only get here once every page has been read: a page that fails raises from
the fetch, before anything is replaced or removed.
The fetch is also unrestricted: a request with a restriction never reaches it, it
goes to Canvas uncached (\cref{sec:subset-listings}), and a listing that an older
copy of the library stored with a restriction is fetched properly before it is
trusted.
That older copy does not remove anything itself, and the entries it adds or
keeps are correct in themselves, so the two copies can share the cache file.
Nor can a positional argument narrow such a fetch: [[get_courses]] and
[[get_assignments]] take keyword arguments only, so one would fail in
[[canvasapi]] before anything is fetched.

An empty answer is the one complete fetch we don't act on.
For a user who has courses, an empty course listing is more likely a passing
state than the truth, a token with fewer rights or a hiccup in Canvas, and
removing every course would cost the whole cache below them, which takes many
requests to rebuild.
The same goes for a course whose assignments all vanish at once.
Keeping them costs little: they stay marked, as before, until a listing that
returns anything at all removes them.

A removed course or assignment takes its cached children with it, the users,
assignments and submissions in its own caches.
Nothing else holds on to one in a way that would break.
A [[LazySubmission]] refers to its assignment, but it lives in that assignment's
cache and goes with it.
Module items and quizzes refer to an assignment by its ID, and fetch it through
the singular method, which then asks Canvas, as it does for any assignment not
cached.
The commands that change assignments add to or remove from the cache with
defaults, so they don't depend on an entry being there.
The individual refresh of a stale object already removed one on a 404
(\cref{sec:cache-copy-to-new}), and this is the same answer from Canvas, given
for a whole listing at once.
Which entries were already left out we must note before the fetch replaces
and marks them.
<<remember which entries were left out before>>=
left_out_before = {key for key, (cached_obj, _) in attr_cache.items()
                   if getattr(cached_obj, "_unlisted", False)}
<<forget entries Canvas did not return, if the listing may>>=
if forget_unlisted and fetched_objs:
  for obj_key in list(attr_cache):
    if (obj_key not in fetched_keys
        and (not confirm_unlisted
             or (obj_key not in left_out_before
                 and canvas_says_it_is_gone(get_attr, self, obj_key)))):
      logger.info(f"Dropped from cache: {attr_name} {cache_key}={obj_key} "
                  f"no longer listed")
      del attr_cache[obj_key]
@

For courses, not being listed is not enough.
What [[get_courses]] lists by default depends on the user's role in each course:
according to Canvas's documentation of the [[state]] argument, a student sees
only available courses, while teachers also see completed ones.
So a course of an earlier round, which someone took as a student or a TA, may
drop out of the listing while it still exists and they still can read it.
Removing it would not only cost its cache: a pattern like [[-c oldround]] would
then find nothing at all, since the listing doesn't have it either.
We could not check against Canvas which courses its listing leaves out.

So the course listing sets [[confirm_unlisted]] as well, and asks Canvas about
each course it did not get back, with the singular method.
Two answers mean that the course is no longer ours, and only then do we remove
it.
A 404, which [[canvasapi]] raises as [[ResourceDoesNotExist]], says that the
course does not exist.
A 403, raised as [[Forbidden]], says that it exists but we may not read it, which
is what a course we have left looks like.
We checked these answers on KTH's Canvas on 9~November 2026, read-only: an
existing course the user is not in gave 403 with the message
\enquote{user not authorised to perform that action}, an ID that does not exist
gave 404, and a public course gave 200.
A rule of 404 alone would thus never have removed a course we left, which is the
commonest way a course leaves the listing.

But a 403 is not always about the course.
Canvas also throttles with a 403, whose message is \enquote{Rate Limit
Exceeded}, and [[canvasapi]] raises [[Forbidden]] for every 403 and
[[RateLimitExceeded]], a subclass of it, for a 429.
Counting every [[Forbidden]] would let a throttled check delete a course and
everything cached below it.
So a 403 counts only if it is not [[RateLimitExceeded]] and its message says
that the user is not authorised, as the answer above did, in any spelling or
case; [[NOT_AUTHORISED]] matches that.
Any other refusal, a throttle among them, keeps the course.
For a 429 [[canvasapi]] makes the message itself, from the rate limit, so it
never matches; the test of the class only states the rule where a reader looks
for it, and holds should that message change.
A course that Canvas still gives us is kept, marked like any entry the listing
did not return.
Any other answer tells us nothing about the course, so it is kept too.
That includes 401, raised as [[Unauthorized]] (or [[InvalidAccessToken]]), which
is about the token, not the course: removing courses because a token has expired
would throw away the cache for a problem the next login fixes.
It also includes other errors from Canvas and no answer at all.
The request is a precaution like those of \cref{sec:course-listing-outage}, so
it gets one try.
We only ask about a course the first time a fetch leaves it out, which costs
one request per course that drops out of the listing.
That is not as rare as it sounds: each round a user took as a student or a TA
may drop out once it ends, and Canvas then still gives it to us, so it is kept,
marked.
Asking about it again at every fetch of the listing, once a day, would cost a
request per such course per day for as long as the cache lives.
The mark tells us that we already asked: the entry was left out by an earlier
fetch, so we leave it be, and a course that Canvas lists again loses the mark.
The price is that a later change goes unnoticed: if we lose access to such a
course after it dropped out, it stays in the cache, marked.
That only leaves a stale entry, which commands still find by name and which
costs nothing until it is read; [[cache clear]] removes it.
A cache written before courses were checked has the marks too, from the marking
of \cref{sec:subset-listings}, so courses it had already left out are not asked
about; they stay as they were before this change.
Assignments don't need this: a teacher's listing of a course's assignments has
all of them, so one missing from it has been deleted.
