\subsection{Refreshing on request}
\label{sec:refresh-on-request}

The time-to-live intervals are a guess about when things change, and sometimes
the user knows better: they were just added to a course, or a grade was just
corrected.
[[--no-cache]] is no answer to that.
It starts from an empty [[Canvas]] object and saves nothing, so it shows the
truth once and leaves the cache as wrong as it was.
Clearing the cache is no better, since it costs the whole hierarchy to fix one
listing.
What we want is the opposite of both: fetch again exactly what this command
reads, and leave the rest of the cache alone.

The hierarchy makes that almost free to express.
A command only reaches the listings it actually walks, so the caching methods
already sit at the right places, and they know how to replace objects without
losing what is cached below them (\cref{sec:cache-copy-to-new}).
All they lack is a way to know that their data is too old, whatever its age.

We could set a flag saying \enquote{refresh}, but then each listing would have
to remember that it has already been refreshed, or a listing read twice by a
command would be fetched twice.
Instead we record \emph{when} the refresh was requested.
Anything fetched before that moment is stale, and anything fetched after it,
including by this run's own refresh, is not.
Every cached object already carries that timestamp, so this needs no new state
in the cache, and the moment itself is not pickled: the next run starts over.
<<functions>>=
refresh_since = None


def request_refresh():
  """Treat everything fetched until now as stale, for the rest of this run."""
  global refresh_since
  refresh_since = datetime.now()


def predates_refresh(fetched_at):
  """Return True if `fetched_at` is from before a requested refresh.

  A missing timestamp (`None`) counts as before.
  """
  if refresh_since is None:
    return False
  return fetched_at is None or fetched_at < refresh_since
@

Four places consult this, one for each way cached data can be returned without
asking Canvas: the plural and singular methods of [[CacheGetMethods]], and the
same two for submissions, where the singular case is the lazy refresh.
That last one checks before it looks at the grade, so that a corrected final
grade shows up: being final is a statement about what changes on its own, not
about what the user just told us has changed.

We deliberately do not put the check in [[outdated]], although that looks like
the natural home.
A bulk fetch keeps most entries Canvas no longer returns, all but the courses and
assignments (\cref{sec:forget-unlisted}), and those it keeps have their old
timestamps.
With the check in [[outdated]], the next listing in the same run would refresh
each of them with a request of its own, and drop the ones that answer with a 404,
so a refresh would behave differently depending on how often a command happens
to read a listing.
For the same reason a refresh adds and updates entries, and removes only what a
complete listing of courses or assignments did not return, as any fetch of those
listings does.

That promise has a consequence for the singular method, which does ask Canvas
about such an entry.
A student who left a course is still in its cached listing and their submissions
are still worth showing, but asking Canvas for that student now gives a 404.
The same read without [[--refresh-cache]] works, from the cache, so the option
must not turn it into a crash: asking for fresher data cannot make data we have
unavailable.
We therefore keep the object, and record that we asked, so that the run does not
ask again.
Only a refresh on request gets this treatment.
A 404 for an object that is not cached, or whose re-fetch the code needs in order
to get data it lacks, is still the caller's to handle.
