\subsection{Refreshing when a name matches nothing}
\label{sec:refresh-on-miss}

A user who types [[-c newcourse]] and is told that no course matches has no
reason to suspect the cache.
So when a pattern matches nothing, we look at the listing once more, this time
at what Canvas says, and search again before giving up.

We have to be careful about what counts as a miss.
An empty result is not one by itself: nothing ungraded, or no TAs in a course, is
a perfectly good answer that a refresh would only slow down.
That also rules out the obvious implementation, which catches [[EmptyListError]]
in [[main]] and runs the command again: it could not tell these cases apart, and
a command that had already written something to Canvas would write it twice.
The retry therefore sits where a name is matched against a listing, and it only
refetches the listing that was searched.

Two limits keep a routine miss cheap.
A listing younger than [[MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES]] is not fetched again,
because it can hardly have the answer yet.
That needs no state of its own: the refresh itself updates the [[_all_fetched]]
timestamp, so a script repeating a typo pays for one listing per interval.
That includes [[ungraded.sh]], whose course pattern may name a round that does not
exist yet: it is a name that matches nothing, so the script pays for one listing
of the courses per interval until the round appears.
It also makes the retry do nothing right after [[--refresh-cache]] or under
[[--no-cache]], when the search just used fresh data.

The other limit is on how many listings one miss may fetch, since each of them
takes at least one request.
How many is reasonable depends on the search.
With [[users -c prgi Anna]] the user has picked the courses and expects Anna to
be in one of them, so the answer is worth waiting for, even if the pattern spans
many course rounds.
With [[users Anna]] the search spans every course they have, and mistyping the
name should not mean fetching every roster.
A search narrowed with [[-c]] may therefore fetch up to
[[MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS]] listings, a search over all courses up to
[[MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES]].
Only the command knows which search it is making, so it passes the latter as
[[max_listings]], using [[miss_refresh_limit]] of the [[courses]] module.
Passing nothing gives the larger limit, which is right for a library caller, who
named the courses it searches.

We cannot know how many courses a user has or how long they are willing to wait,
so 25 and 5 are only defaults, and the environment variables below set them.
A limit of 0 turns the retry off for that kind of search, for instance for a
script that would rather have a quick answer.
[[filter_courses]] searches the course listing with the larger limit, so setting
[[CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS]] to 0 stops looking for new courses too.

The limit stands for the cost of the refresh, so it counts the listings that are
old enough to be fetched and not all that were searched.
Otherwise a search over 30 courses where 3 rosters are old would be refused,
although it costs 3 listings.
It would also complain right after [[--refresh-cache]] or under [[--no-cache]],
when there is nothing left to fetch and the answer is already based on what
Canvas says.
Over the limit nothing is fetched at all.
Fetching only some of the listings would cost time and still not tell whether
the name exists, and the user could not know which courses had been looked at.

When the limit stops a refresh, we warn about it.
This used to be silent, and that was wrong: the command goes on to report that
nothing matched, in the same words as when it did look, and the user cannot tell
\enquote{not there} from \enquote{did not look}.
Only the second calls for doing something, so the warning names both ways to do
it: [[--refresh-cache]] for this time, and the variable of the limit that
applied for every time.
Since it goes through logging it ends up on stderr, away from the TSV on stdout;
[[-q]] hides it, and a library caller that has not configured logging still gets
it from Python's handler of last resort.
A script repeating such a search is warned on every run, because nothing was
fetched and the listings stay old: its answer is as uncertain every time.
A limit of 0 is different, there the user has asked for no retry and needs no
reminder, so we only log that at the [[INFO]] level.

A pattern that matches something is not a miss at all, even when the listing is
stale, so [[-c prgi]] can still silently leave out a new course that the old one
hides.
This is why this retry complements the time-to-live and [[--refresh-cache]]
rather than replacing them.
<<constants>>=
MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES = env_number(
  "CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MINUTES", 10, int)
MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS = env_number(
  "CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS", 25, int)
MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES = env_number(
  "CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES", 5, int)
<<functions>>=
def retry_after_miss(find, listings, max_listings=None, pattern=None,
                     stop_at_first_match=False):
  """Yield from `find()`; if empty, refresh `listings` and search again.

  `find` returns an iterable of matches, and `listings` holds the
  `(owner, attribute)` pairs it searched, such as `(canvas, "course")`. Only a
  listing that is old enough is refreshed. They are all refreshed before
  searching again, or with `stop_at_first_match` one at a time in the order
  given, searching again after each and stopping at the first that gives a
  match. If more than `max_listings` are old enough, which defaults to
  `MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS`, none is refreshed and a warning says so; 0 turns
  the refresh off without a warning. `pattern`, what was searched for, is only
  used in the messages. A failed refresh leaves that listing as it was cached.
  """
  found = False
  for match in find():
    found = True
    yield match
  if found:
    return

  <<find the [[old_listings]] among [[listings]]>>
  if not old_listings:
    return

  <<give up unless the limit allows [[old_listings]]>>
  <<say that the refresh may take a while>>

  if stop_at_first_match:
    batches = [[listing] for listing in old_listings]
  else:
    batches = [old_listings]
  for batch in batches:
    <<expire the listings of [[batch]]>>
    try:
      with without_retries(owner for owner, _, _ in batch):
        matches = list(find())
    except (canvasapi.exceptions.CanvasException,
            requests.exceptions.RequestException) as error:
      logger.warning(f"Could not refresh after finding no match: {error}")
      return
    finally:
      <<keep the cached listings of [[batch]] after a failed refresh>>
    if matches:
      yield from matches
      return
@

The default pattern of an option, [[.*]], does not name anything, so a search
with it that finds nothing has not missed anything either: there is just no one
with that role, say.
Callers use this helper to leave their [[listings]] empty in that case.
<<functions>>=
def pattern_names_something(pattern):
  """Return True if the regex `pattern` was given to find something specific."""
  return bool(pattern) and pattern != ".*"
@

Not every search can be handed to [[retry_after_miss]] as it is.
The lookups of pages, modules and files take one course, and the commands call
them in a loop over the selected courses, since they do something for each
course in between.
A retry inside such a lookup would make every course a search of its own: a page
that exists in one of twelve course rounds is missing from the other eleven, and
each of them would fetch its listing, on every run after the minimum age, with
no limit across the courses.
What counts as a miss is the name missing from the whole selection.
Such a command therefore asks once, before its loop, with a search over all the
courses, and only for the effect on the cache: after a refresh, the loop finds
the new entry in the cached listings and makes no further request.
The search can stop at the first match, since one match is enough to know that
there was no miss.
<<functions>>=
def refresh_after_miss(pattern, find, listings, max_listings=None):
  """Refresh `listings` if `pattern` names something that `find()` lacks.

  For a search that goes owner by owner: call this once before it, with a
  `find` that searches all the owners. The arguments are those of
  `retry_after_miss`; a `pattern` that names nothing never refreshes.
  """
  if pattern_names_something(pattern):
    for _ in retry_after_miss(find, listings, max_listings=max_listings,
                              pattern=pattern):
      break
@

We only look at timestamps that are actually [[datetime]]s, since an owner that
has no such listing, like an assignment group, has nothing to refresh.
<<find the [[old_listings]] among [[listings]]>>=
old_listings = []
for owner, attribute in listings:
  name = f"{attribute}_all_fetched"
  fetched = getattr(owner, name, None)
  if old_enough_to_ask_again(fetched):
    old_listings.append((owner, name, fetched))
<<functions>>=
def old_enough_to_ask_again(fetched):
  """Returns True if a fetch at `fetched` is too long ago to go on trusting."""
  return (isinstance(fetched, datetime)
          and datetime.now() - fetched
              >= timedelta(minutes=MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES))
@

We look up the default limit here rather than in the signature, where it would
be fixed when the module is loaded.
No search can stay within a negative limit, so we take it to mean the same as 0
rather than warn about being over a limit of $-1$.
<<give up unless the limit allows [[old_listings]]>>=
limit = MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS if max_listings is None else max_listings
if limit <= 0:
  logger.info("No match in the cache; the refresh is turned off "
              f"(limit {limit})")
  return
if len(old_listings) > limit:
  <<warn that the limit stops the refresh>>
  return
@

The variable to name follows from the limit that applied.
A caller may also pass a limit of its own, which no variable can raise, and then
the warning must not suggest one.
<<warn that the limit stops the refresh>>=
ways_out = "--refresh-cache"
if max_listings is None:
  ways_out += ", or raise CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS"
elif max_listings == MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES:
  ways_out += ", or raise CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES"
logger.warning(f"No match in the cache; not fetching {len(old_listings)} "
               f"listings as the limit is {limit}.\nUse {ways_out}.")
@

A refresh costs a request or more per listing; issue~\#583 measured roughly 7
to 16~seconds per roster on one account, a few runs and not a benchmark.
For a person, most of that is wasted: a student who is new is expected in one of
the selected courses, so once a roster has them, the others can't add anything
the search is looking for.
So a caller can ask, with [[stop_at_first_match]], for the listings to be
refreshed one at a time, in its order, with a search after each, stopping at the
first that gives a match.
A student who is new in one of 12 courses is then found after the refresh of
their course, and the rosters after it are not fetched at all.
The cost is that the search runs once per refreshed listing, but apart from the
listing just expired it reads the cache and makes no request.
Only a name that is really absent, or a typo, still refreshes every listing,
up to the limit; we have no way to tell that from a name Canvas has but the
cache lacks.

Only [[filter_users]] asks for this.
An assignment is not like a person: \enquote{Lab 5} added to a course is
usually added to its other rounds as well, and the command must list it from
each of them, so stopping at the first would give a partial answer that looks
complete.
The assignment lookup therefore refreshes every old listing before searching
again, as does a library caller that does not say otherwise.
The course lookup searches a single listing, where the two are the same.
Both ways go through the same loop, over batches of one listing or over a single
batch of them all.

Clearing the timestamps is what makes the next search fetch the listings.
The others are left as they are, so the search reads them from the cache.
<<expire the listings of [[batch]]>>=
for owner, name, _ in batch:
  logger.info(f"No match, refreshing listing: {name}")
  setattr(owner, name, None)
@

A refresh of several listings can take minutes, and until now the command said
nothing while it waited, which looks like a hang.
So when more than one listing may be fetched, we say so first, with the pattern
and the number.
That is a warning, since it explains a delay the user will notice, and it goes
through the logger like the warning about the limit: on stderr, away from the
output, and hidden by [[-q]], as a script run by [[cron]] wants.
When the refresh stops at the first match we say \enquote{up to}.
A single listing is quick enough to go without a word, and that is the case of
the course listing, which every command reads through [[filter_courses]].
<<say that the refresh may take a while>>=
if len(old_listings) > 1:
  what = f" for '{pattern}'" if pattern else ""
  up_to = "up to " if stop_at_first_match else ""
  logger.warning(f"No match{what} in the cache; checking {up_to}"
                 f"{len(old_listings)} listings of "
                 f"{kinds_in_words(old_listings)} in Canvas")
@

The message names what is listed, in the plural.
Adding an s to the attribute name does for most kinds, but not for a quiz or a
group category, and a search for a quiz reads two listings per course, one of
Classic Quizzes and one of New Quizzes (\cref{sec:caching-quizzes}).
So we name every kind among the listings, in the order they come.
<<constants>>=
LISTING_PLURALS = {
  "quiz": "Classic Quizzes",
  "new_quiz": "New Quizzes",
  "group_category": "group categories",
}
<<functions>>=
def kinds_in_words(old_listings):
  """Return the kinds of `old_listings` in the plural, as `users and groups`."""
  kinds = []
  for _, name, _ in old_listings:
    kind = name.removesuffix("_all_fetched")
    words = LISTING_PLURALS.get(kind, kind.replace("_", " ") + "s")
    if words not in kinds:
      kinds.append(words)
  return " and ".join(kinds)
@

The refresh is a favour, so failing at it should not make things worse.
Without it the user would have been told that nothing matched, and that is still
what they get, along with a warning, since the answer may now be wrong.
For the same reason it gets one try (\cref{sec:course-listing-outage}), so the
user does not wait 17 minutes to hear that nothing matched.
A failure we see stops the refresh: Canvas is then most likely down, and trying
each of the remaining listings would cost a try each to learn the same.
We restore the timestamp because an expired listing would otherwise be fetched
by the next command, which might well have found its match in the cache, and
fail on Canvas being down.

We cannot rely on seeing the exception to know that a refresh failed:
[[list_users]] catches [[CanvasException]] itself, to go on with the other courses.
What tells us is the timestamp, since a listing that was fetched has a new one.
That is why this is in a [[finally]] clause and looks at each listing, which
also keeps what a partly successful refresh of several did get.
Such a failure, of one course's roster, does not stop a refresh one at a time,
just as [[list_users]] itself goes on with the other courses, and the listings
refreshed before it keep what they got.
<<keep the cached listings of [[batch]] after a failed refresh>>=
for owner, name, fetched in batch:
  if getattr(owner, name) is None:
    setattr(owner, name, fetched)
@

Let's verify the behaviour, again with a stand-in whose listing can change
between the calls.

<<test functions>>=
class TestRetryAfterMiss:
    """Test when a search that found nothing looks at Canvas again"""

    def listing_owner(self, age=timedelta(hours=1), listing=(2,)):
        """Return an owner of a listing cached `age` ago, with item 1."""
        class TestClass:
            listings = 0
            fail = None
            def get_item(self, item_id, **kwargs):
                return canvas_object(id=item_id)
            def get_items(self, **kwargs):
                if self.fail:
                    raise self.fail
                self.listings += 1
                return [canvas_object(id=each) for each in self.items]

        CacheGetMethods("item")(TestClass)
        owner = TestClass()
        owner.items = [1]
        list(owner.get_items())
        owner.item_all_fetched = datetime.now() - age
        owner.items = listing
        return owner

    def search(self, owner, wanted=2):
        return list(retry_after_miss(
            lambda: (obj.id for obj in owner.get_items() if obj.id == wanted),
            [(owner, "item")]))

    def test_new_item_is_found_with_one_more_listing(self):
        owner = self.listing_owner()
        assert self.search(owner) == [2]
        assert owner.listings == 2

    def test_a_match_does_not_look_at_canvas(self):
        owner = self.listing_owner()
        assert self.search(owner, wanted=1) == [1]
        assert owner.listings == 1

    def test_repeated_miss_lists_once_per_interval(self):
        owner = self.listing_owner()
        assert self.search(owner, wanted=3) == []
        assert self.search(owner, wanted=3) == []
        assert owner.listings == 2

    def test_a_young_listing_is_not_fetched_again(self):
        owner = self.listing_owner(age=timedelta(minutes=1))
        assert self.search(owner) == []
        assert owner.listings == 1

    def test_a_connection_error_reports_no_match(self, caplog):
        """The likeliest outage is not a CanvasException."""
        owner = self.listing_owner()
        fetched = owner.item_all_fetched
        owner.fail = requests.exceptions.ConnectionError("Unreachable")

        with caplog.at_level(logging.WARNING):
            assert self.search(owner) == []

        assert owner.item_all_fetched == fetched
        assert "Could not refresh" in caplog.text

    @pytest.mark.parametrize("pattern,expected", [
        (None, False), ("", False), (".*", False), ("Anna", True)])
    def test_only_a_given_pattern_names_something(self, pattern, expected):
        assert pattern_names_something(pattern) is expected

    def refresh_for(self, owners, pattern, wanted, **kwargs):
        """Do what a command does before it searches `owners` one by one."""
        refresh_after_miss(
            pattern,
            lambda: (obj for owner in owners for obj in owner.get_items()
                     if obj.id == wanted),
            [(owner, "item") for owner in owners], **kwargs)

    def test_a_name_missing_from_every_owner_refreshes_them(self):
        owners = [self.listing_owner(), self.listing_owner(listing=(1,))]

        self.refresh_for(owners, "two", wanted=2)

        assert [owner.listings for owner in owners] == [2, 2]
        assert 2 in [obj.id for obj in owners[0].get_items()]
        assert [owner.listings for owner in owners] == [2, 2]

    def test_a_name_that_one_owner_has_refreshes_none(self):
        owners = [self.listing_owner(), self.listing_owner()]

        self.refresh_for(owners, "one", wanted=1)

        assert [owner.listings for owner in owners] == [1, 1]

    @pytest.mark.parametrize("pattern", [None, "", ".*"])
    def test_a_pattern_that_names_nothing_refreshes_none(self, pattern):
        owner = self.listing_owner()

        self.refresh_for([owner], pattern, wanted=3)

        assert owner.listings == 1

    def test_the_refresh_before_a_loop_keeps_to_the_limit(self):
        owners = [self.listing_owner(), self.listing_owner()]

        self.refresh_for(owners, "two", wanted=2, max_listings=1)

        assert [owner.listings for owner in owners] == [1, 1]

    def test_too_many_listings_are_not_fetched(self):
        owners = [self.listing_owner()
