\subsection{What goes into the cache file}
\label{sec:state-filters}

So far the cache file holds the objects exactly as they are in memory:
[[cli/cache.nw]] pickles the [[Canvas]] object, and [[pickle]] stores every
attribute of every object below it.
That is more than the file needs.
Some attributes are large and never read again, and some listings have not been
used for months (issue~\#586); some hold what a user doesn't want on disk at all
(issue~\#578).
The objects in memory must stay as they are, though: a command that just fetched
a roster uses all of it, whatever the file is going to keep.

[[pickle]] has a hook for this difference.
Before it stores an object it asks the object's class for the state to store,
through [[__getstate__]], and stores that in place of the attributes.
We give a class that hook once, and let it run a list of \emph{state filters}.
A filter is a function [[state_filter(obj, state)]] that changes [[state]], a
copy of the object's attributes, in place.
The stored state remains a dictionary of attributes, so nothing changes for
whoever reads the file: unpickling sets what is there, and a copy of this
library without the filters reads the file as before.
A filter must therefore leave an attribute that the code reads without a
default in place, emptied if need be, and not remove it.

The filters must not run every time an object is pickled or copied.
[[copy.copy]] asks [[__getstate__]] too, and a program using this library may
pickle these objects for reasons of its own; neither should get a filtered
object.
So the filters run only inside [[writing_cache_file]], the context that
[[cli/cache.nw]] enters while it pickles for the file.
Outside it, the hook returns the attributes themselves, which is what [[pickle]]
uses for an object without a hook.
The context is a [[ContextVar]], so pickling in one thread does not filter what
another thread copies meanwhile.

A filter for a class also applies to its subclasses, base class first, and the
filters of one class run in the order they were added.
The hook is ours alone, though.
A class that already decides its state with a [[__getstate__]] of its own cannot
get filters, since ours would replace it without anyone noticing, so we refuse;
and a subclass that defines one bypasses the filters of its base classes, which
we cannot see from here.
None of the [[canvasapi]] classes has one.
<<functions>>=
state_filters = {}
writing_to_cache_file = contextvars.ContextVar("writing_to_cache_file",
                                               default=False)

@contextlib.contextmanager
def writing_cache_file():
  """Let the state filters apply to what is pickled inside the `with` block."""
  token = writing_to_cache_file.set(True)
  try:
    yield
  finally:
    writing_to_cache_file.reset(token)

def add_state_filter(cls, state_filter):
  """Let `state_filter` change what the cache file stores of a `cls` object.

  It is called as `state_filter(obj, state)` with a copy of the attributes of
  `obj`, to change in place, each time such an object is pickled inside
  `writing_cache_file`. It must not change `obj`.
  """
  if cls not in state_filters:
    if vars(cls).get("__getstate__", state_for_pickle) is not state_for_pickle:
      raise TypeError(f"{cls.__name__} has a __getstate__ of its own, "
                      f"which the state filters would replace")
    state_filters[cls] = []
    cls.__getstate__ = state_for_pickle
  state_filters[cls].append(state_filter)

def state_for_pickle(self):
  """Return the attributes to pickle for `self`, filtered for the cache file."""
  if not writing_to_cache_file.get():
    return self.__dict__
  state = self.__dict__.copy()
  for cls in reversed(type(self).__mro__):
    for state_filter in state_filters.get(cls, ()):
      state_filter(self, state)
  return state
@

Let's verify the hook on classes of our own, so that no filter of a test stays
on a real class.
A filter must change what is pickled for the file and nothing else: not the
object in memory, not a copy of it and not a pickle made outside the context.

<<test functions>>=
class Secretive(CanvasObject):
    """A stand-in whose `secret` is left out of the cache file."""

class Recording(Secretive):
    """A subclass whose own filter records the `secret` it was given."""

class Ordered(CanvasObject):
    """A stand-in with two filters that note the order they ran in."""

def without_secret(obj, state):
    state["secret"] = None

def seen_secret(obj, state):
    state["seen"] = state["secret"]

def first(obj, state):
    state["order"] = ["first"]

def second(obj, state):
    state["order"] = state.get("order", []) + ["second"]

add_state_filter(Secretive, without_secret)
add_state_filter(Recording, seen_secret)
add_state_filter(Ordered, first)
add_state_filter(Ordered, second)

def pickled_for_file(obj):
    """Return `obj` as it comes back from the cache file."""
    with writing_cache_file():
        pickled = pickle.dumps(obj)
    return pickle.loads(pickled)

class TestStateFilters:
    """Test what the cache file stores of an object with state filters"""

    def test_filter_applies_to_the_file_only(self):
        thing = Secretive(None, {"name": "x", "secret": "s"})

        stored = pickled_for_file(thing)

        assert (stored.name, stored.secret) == ("x", None)
        assert thing.secret == "s"
        assert copy.copy(thing).secret == "s"
        assert pickle.loads(pickle.dumps(thing)).secret == "s"

    def test_filters_stop_when_the_writing_fails(self):
        thing = Secretive(None, {"secret": "s"})

        with pytest.raises(RuntimeError):
            with writing_cache_file():
                raise RuntimeError("disk full")

        assert pickle.loads(pickle.dumps(thing)).secret == "s"

    def test_subclass_gets_the_filters_of_its_base_class_first(self):
        stored = pickled_for_file(Recording(None, {"secret": "s"}))

        assert (stored.secret, stored.seen) == (None, None)

    def test_filters_of_a_class_run_in_the_order_added(self):
        assert pickled_for_file(Ordered(None, {})).order == ["first", "second"]

    def test_a_class_with_its_own_state_hook_is_refused(self):
        class Stateful(CanvasObject):
            def __getstate__(self):
                return {}

        with pytest.raises(TypeError, match="Stateful"):
            add_state_filter(Stateful, without_secret)
        assert Stateful not in canvaslms.hacks.canvasapi.state_filters
@


\subsubsection{Enrollments, shortened}
\label{sec:short-enrollments}

The first filter is for the largest thing in the file.
We fetch every roster with [[include=["enrollments"]]], since the enrollments
say who is a student and who is a teacher (the role filter of [[users]] reads
their [[type]]).
Canvas answers with 26 fields for each enrollment: grades, activity times,
identifiers of the student system, links.
In a real cache of 176 courses with 92\,000 cached users, those enrollments were
43 of the pickle's 72~MB, for the sake of one field.

So the file keeps a short list of fields, [[ENROLLMENT_FIELDS]]: what tells who
someone is in a course and in which section, and nothing about how they are
doing.
The filter shortens the enrollments in the state and leaves the user in memory
alone.

A shortened entry must say that it is shortened.
The cache trusts its record of what each entry was fetched with, and that record
says [[enrollments]]; if the next reader needs a field we dropped, the record
would lie to it.
The filter therefore also stores which fields it kept, as
[[_enrollment_fields]] on the user.
An entry without that attribute has everything Canvas sent, which is true for a
freshly fetched user and, with one exception that we return to below, for one
written by a copy of this library that has no filter.
A new attribute that is read with a default needs no new schema version.
When an entry that was already short is stored again, the record cannot grow:
it becomes the fields that both the entry and this process keep.
<<constants>>=
ENROLLMENT_FIELDS = frozenset({
  "id", "user_id", "course_id", "course_section_id",
  "type", "role", "enrollment_state",
})
<<functions>>=
def short_enrollments(user, state):
  """State filter: keep only `ENROLLMENT_FIELDS` of each enrollment.

  Records the fields that may be left as `_enrollment_fields`.
  """
  enrollments = state.get("enrollments")
  if (ENROLLMENT_FIELDS is None or not isinstance(enrollments, list)
      or state.get("_unlisted")):
    return
  kept = ENROLLMENT_FIELDS & state.get("_enrollment_fields", ENROLLMENT_FIELDS)
  state["enrollments"] = [
    {field: value for field, value in enrollment.items() if field in kept}
    if isinstance(enrollment, dict) else enrollment
    for enrollment in enrollments]
  state["_enrollment_fields"] = kept

def make_stored_enrollments_short():
  add_state_filter(canvasapi.user.User, short_enrollments)
@

The other half is to use the record.
Whether a cached entry has what a call asks for is decided by [[must_update]],
from the keyword arguments the entry was fetched with.
We give it those arguments as the file kept them: if the entry's enrollments
lack a field that this process keeps, the entry no longer counts as fetched with
[[enrollments]].
A call that asks for enrollments then fetches again, with everything the entry
was ever fetched with, and gets a full object without the mark.
A call that doesn't ask for them is answered from the cache as before.

Which fields a process needs is [[ENROLLMENT_FIELDS]] again, so the two halves
cannot disagree: what we would store is what we are content to read.
That also makes the list safe to extend.
When a later version reads one more field and adds it here, the entries stored
without it count as lacking, and each roster is fetched once more.

Someone who has left the course is the exception, on both sides.
Canvas no longer lists them, so no fetch can bring back what we drop
(\cref{sec:forget-unlisted}).
The filter therefore leaves a user marked [[_unlisted]] as they are, which
costs little, since they are few.
One who was stored short before leaving stays short, and must not count as
lacking: nothing can repair them, so every read would fetch the roster in vain,
again and again.
[[kept_kwargs]] therefore takes such an entry at its word.
A caller that needs more fields gets that one user with fewer.

The record has one more limit.
A copy of this library from before the filters keeps a short entry's
enrollments but not its mark, in the rare case where its listing replaces an
entry that was fetched alone with more than the listing asks for.
What it writes back is then short and unmarked, and we take it for complete
until a process with the filter stores it again.
With the default list this changes nothing, since everything we read is in it.
A program that uses this library and reads other fields can set
[[ENROLLMENT_FIELDS]] to a larger set, or to [[None]] for everything, before it
reads; it then also stores what it asked for.
It shares the file with programs that shorten, though, so it fetches a roster
again whenever one of them has saved it in between.
<<functions>>=
def kept_kwargs(obj, prev_kwargs):
  """Return `prev_kwargs` without what the cache file kept only part of.

  `prev_kwargs` are the keyword arguments the cached `obj` was fetched with.
  """
  kept = getattr(obj, "_enrollment_fields", None)
  if (kept is None or "include" not in prev_kwargs
      or getattr(obj, "_unlisted", False)
      or (ENROLLMENT_FIELDS is not None and ENROLLMENT_FIELDS <= kept)):
    return prev_kwargs
  return {**prev_kwargs,
          "include": [include for include in as_list(prev_kwargs["include"])
                      if include != "enrollments"]}
@

The mark starts with an underscore, and attributes of ours are not copied from
an old object to the one that replaces it
(\cref{sec:what-a-listing-asks-for}).
That is right when the new object brings enrollments of its own.
But a listing that doesn't ask for enrollments keeps the old object's, if the
entry had been fetched with them, and those are still short.
The mark must follow the enrollments it describes.
<<carry over the mark of short enrollments>>=
if (hasattr(old_obj, "_enrollment_fields")
    and getattr(obj, "enrollments", None)
        is getattr(old_obj, "enrollments", None)):
  obj._enrollment_fields = old_obj._enrollment_fields
@

Let's verify this on a stand-in for a course, whose one user Canvas sends with a
full enrollment when asked to.
The stand-in counts the requests, and since it is stored with the rest we can
ask the copy that comes back from the file.
For the last two tests one entry must have been fetched with more than the
listing asks for, since that is when the new object takes over attributes of
the old one: the listing is fetched first and then the user alone with more.
In the first of them the listing fetches no enrollments, so the old, short ones
are kept and their mark with them; in the second it fetches them, and the full
enrollments must not get the mark.

<<test functions>>=
FULL_ENROLLMENT = {
    "id": 7, "user_id": 1, "course_id": 1, "course_section_id": 3,
    "type": "StudentEnrollment", "role": "StudentEnrollment",
    "enrollment_state": "active",
    "grades": {"current_grade": "A"},
