\subsubsection{Courses nobody has used for a long time}
\label{sec:forget-unused-contents}

Nothing so far leaves the cache unless Canvas stops listing it.
A roster that was fetched once, because a search went through every course,
stays in the file for good, and every later command loads it.
The second filter leaves out the contents of a course that has not been used for
[[CACHE_KEEP_DAYS]] days.

We cannot see what has been \emph{used}.
A read that the cache answers leaves no trace, and it should not: a record of
reads would have to be saved, and a command that only reads would write the
whole file again (\cref{sec:skip-unchanged-save}).
What we do have is when each object was \emph{fetched}.
That is nearly as good, since a listing in use is fetched again whenever it
expires, but only nearly: a submission with a passing grade never expires
(\cref{sec:cache-ttl-constants}), so [[results]] for last year's course reads
submissions that were fetched a year ago.
Judging each listing by its own age would drop exactly those while they are in
use.

So the unit is the course.
Any use of a course fetches something below it sooner or later, its roster
every other day and its list of assignments every week, even when most of what
is read is old.
If nothing below a course has been fetched for [[CACHE_KEEP_DAYS]] days, the
course has not been used for about as long.
The course's own time stamp does not count: the list of courses is fetched
every day, whether or not anyone looks at this course.
An entry without a time stamp, such as an assignment that [[assignments create]]
put in the cache itself, tells nothing; a course with only such entries is
kept.
<<constants>>=
CACHE_KEEP_DAYS = env_number("CANVASLMS_CACHE_KEEP_DAYS", 90, int)
<<functions>>=
def newest_fetch_below(obj):
  """Return when something cached below `obj` was last fetched, or None."""
  try:
    attributes = object.__getattribute__(obj, "__dict__")
  except AttributeError:
    return None
  newest = None
  for name, cache in attributes.items():
    if not (name.endswith("_cache") and isinstance(cache, dict)):
      continue
    for entry in cache.values():
      child = entry[0] if isinstance(entry, tuple) else entry
      for fetched in (fetch_time(child), newest_fetch_below(child)):
        if fetched and (newest is None or fetched > newest):
          newest = fetched
  return newest

def fetch_time(obj):
  """Return when the cached `obj` was fetched, or None if it doesn't say."""
  try:
    attributes = object.__getattribute__(obj, "__dict__")
  except AttributeError:
    return None
  return attributes.get("_fetched_at") or attributes.get("_last_refresh")
@

We read the attributes through [[object.__getattribute__]], because the
submissions in an assignment's cache are [[LazySubmission]] wrappers
(\cref{sec:lazy-submission}), and asking one of those for an attribute in the
ordinary way can make it fetch from Canvas.
Their time stamp is [[_last_refresh]].

What we leave out is everything cached below the course: each of its caches is
empty in the file and each listing counts as not fetched, so the next command
that reads one fetches it.
With the assignments go their submissions, and with the roster the users who
have left the course, whom the cache otherwise keeps for the sake of their
results (\cref{sec:forget-unlisted}).
That is the price, and it is why the limit is long and can be set:
[[CANVASLMS_CACHE_KEEP_DAYS]] is 90 days unless set otherwise, and 0 keeps
everything for good.
The course itself stays, so the list of courses is still complete, and so does
the record of what each of its listings asks for, [[_all_kwargs]], so the next
fetch brings what the last one did.

Like every state filter, this one changes the file and not the objects in
memory, and it runs only when the file is written anyway.
A command cannot lose what it is working with: what it fetched has a new time
stamp.
<<functions>>=
def forget_unused_contents(course, state):
  """State filter: leave out the contents of a course unused for a long time."""
  if CACHE_KEEP_DAYS <= 0:
    return
  newest = newest_fetch_below(course)
  if newest is None or datetime.now() - newest <= timedelta(days=CACHE_KEEP_DAYS):
    return
  for name, value in state.items():
    if name.endswith("_cache") and isinstance(value, dict):
      state[name] = {}
    elif name.endswith("_all_fetched"):
      state[name] = None
  <<say that the contents of [[course]] were left out>>

def make_unused_course_contents_forgotten():
  import canvasapi.course
  add_state_filter(canvasapi.course.Course, forget_unused_contents)
@

Most of what we leave out can be fetched again, so saying it in the log, for
whoever runs with [[-v]], is enough.
The users who had left the course cannot, and their results were the reason to
keep them.
Losing them deserves a warning, which names the setting that prevents it.
It comes once for a course, since the next file has nothing left to lose.
<<say that the contents of [[course]] were left out>>=
left = sum(1 for entry in getattr(course, "user_cache", {}).values()
           if isinstance(entry, tuple) and getattr(entry[0], "_unlisted", False))
message = (f"Cache pruning: nothing in course {state.get('id')} was fetched "
           f"since {newest:%Y-%m-%d}, so the cache file leaves out what was "
           f"cached of it")
if left:
  logger.warning(f"{message}, including {count_noun(left, 'user')} who had "
                 f"left the course; CANVASLMS_CACHE_KEEP_DAYS=0 keeps "
                 f"everything")
else:
  logger.info(message)
<<functions>>=
def count_noun(count, noun):
  """Return `count` with `noun`, in the plural unless the count is 1."""
  return f"{count} {noun}" if count == 1 else f"{count} {noun}s"
