\subsubsection{Kinds the user keeps out of the file}
\label{sec:cache-exclude}

By pickling the [[Canvas]] object, we persist everything below it in the
hierarchy.
Besides course content, that includes personal data: the participant list of
every course ([[Course.user_cache]]), the members of every group
([[Group.user_cache]]), the groups themselves, whose names often are student
names, and the submissions we have fetched, with their history, comments and
grades when those are included.

Some users prefer not to keep student data on their machine, even in the
encrypted cache file, while still benefiting from the caching of course content
(issue~\#578).
The third filter is for them: it leaves the kinds of objects they name out of
the file.
A kind is the attribute name that the caching decorators use, so [[user]]
covers the participants of a course as well as the members of a group:
\begin{minted}{bash}
CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE=user,group,submission canvaslms pages edit ...
CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE=personal canvaslms pages edit ...
\end{minted}
The second form uses the alias [[personal]], which stands for the kinds that
hold personal data.
The alias is the recommended form: the user states the intent, and when a
future version caches a new kind of personal data, adding it to the alias
protects everyone who already uses it.
It also covers what a single kind does not.
A group that was fetched with its members included carries their names itself,
and a submission names its student in what Canvas sends, so excluding [[user]]
alone leaves those in the file.

Excluded objects are still cached in memory during the run, so a single
command behaves exactly as before.
Only the next run notices the difference: it must fetch the excluded objects
again.
That is the price of the option, and the reason it is opt-in.

\paragraph{Reading the setting}
The setting is a set of names, which the numeric [[env_number]] cannot read.
A sibling helper splits the value on commas and expands aliases.
We normalize case and whitespace, since [[User, Submission]] is an
unambiguous request.
The command line and the configuration file give us the same kind of value
(\cref{sec:cache-exclude-sources}), sometimes as a list already, so the parsing
is a function of its own that takes either.
<<environment helpers>>=
def names_in(value, aliases):
  """Return the set of lower-case names in `value`.

  `value` is a comma-separated string or an iterable of such strings. Each
  name in `aliases` is replaced by the set of names it stands for.
  """
  items = [value] if isinstance(value, str) else value
  names = set()
  for item in ",".join(items).split(","):
    item = item.strip().lower()
    if item:
      names |= aliases.get(item, {item})
  return names

def env_names(name, aliases):
  """Read the comma-separated setting `name` from the environment."""
  return names_in(os.environ.get(name, ""), aliases)
@

The kinds holding personal data are users (course participants and group
members), groups and submissions.
We leave out group categories: their names describe a grouping, such as
\enquote{Lab groups}, not the students in it.
The alias [[none]] names no kind at all.
It is there for the more specific sources of the setting, so that a command
line can turn off what the configuration file turned on.
<<constants>>=
PERSONAL_DATA_KINDS = frozenset({"user", "group", "submission"})
KIND_ALIASES = {"personal": PERSONAL_DATA_KINDS, "none": frozenset()}
CACHE_EXCLUDE = frozenset(env_names("CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE", KIND_ALIASES))
<<functions>>=
def exclude_from_cache_file(kinds):
  """Leave the `kinds` of objects out of the cache file from now on.

  `kinds` is a comma-separated string or an iterable of names, as in
  CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE, which this replaces. Returns the names that are no
  kind that is cached, sorted, for the caller to report.
  """
  global CACHE_EXCLUDE
  CACHE_EXCLUDE = frozenset(names_in(kinds, KIND_ALIASES))
  return unknown_kinds(CACHE_EXCLUDE)
@

\paragraph{Leaving a kind out}
Each caching decorator knows the attributes it keeps its cache in, so it is
the one to register the filter for its kind, on the class it decorates.
That works wherever in the hierarchy the object sits: users hang off both
courses and groups, and groups off both courses and group categories.

The filter must be careful about what it leaves out.
Unpickling never runs [[__init__]], so an object restored without its cache
attributes would crash on the first cached call.
Instead of dropping the attributes, we reset them to the values the
constructor gives them.
All of them matter.
If we emptied only the cache and kept the [[_all_fetched]] timestamp, the
restored object would believe that it holds the complete listing, and the
plural method would return an empty list instead of fetching it.
Each object needs an empty value of its own, too: [[pickle]] stores an object
it meets twice only once, so one shared empty dictionary in the states would
come back as one dictionary shared by every course.
The decorator therefore gives us, for each attribute, a function that makes
the empty value.

We test for exclusion when pickling, not when decorating, so that the setting
can change after the decorators have run.
We also record every kind that the decorators cache, so that we can tell a
misspelled kind from a real one.
<<constants>>=
CACHED_KINDS = set()
<<functions>>=
def leave_out_of_cache_file(cls, kind, emptied):
  """Give `cls` a state filter that leaves `kind` out when it is excluded.

  `emptied` maps the attributes that hold the cache of `kind` to functions
  that return their empty values.
  """
  CACHED_KINDS.add(kind)

  def without_kind(obj, state):
    if kind in CACHE_EXCLUDE:
      for name, empty in emptied.items():
        state[name] = empty()

  add_state_filter(cls, without_kind)

def nothing():
  """Return None, the value of a listing that has not been fetched."""
  return None
@

The general decorator, [[CacheGetMethods]], names its attributes after the
kind it caches.
We add to its chunk [[<<create decorator functions and update [[cls]]>>]],
which \cref{sec:cache-get-methods-decorator} starts.
The record of what the listing asks for, [[_all_kwargs]], stays: it names
includes, not people, and the next fetch should bring what the last one did.
<<create decorator functions and update [[cls]]>>=
leave_out_of_cache_file(cls, attr_name, {
  f"{attr_name}_cache": dict,
  f"{attr_name}_all_fetched": nothing,
})
@

The submissions decorator uses its own attribute names: its constructor
extension stores the cache in [[__cache]] and the timestamp in
[[__all_fetched]].
Since the decorator is a function, not a class, Python does not mangle these
names, so they are the literal attribute names.
It has a third attribute, [[__unlisted]], with the identifiers of the students
whose submissions Canvas no longer lists (\cref{sec:forget-unlisted}).
Those are students too, so it is emptied with the rest.
<<decorator body for caching assignment submissions>>=
leave_out_of_cache_file(cls, "submission", {
  "__cache": dict,
  "__all_fetched": nothing,
  "__unlisted": set,
})
@

\paragraph{What a command attached to a submission}
Emptying the caches is not enough, because an excluded object can also sit on
an object that is kept.
The commands attach the student to each submission they work with
([[submission.user]], set by [[filter_submissions]]), and the wrapper passes
that on to the submission it wraps, which is what we store
(\cref{sec:lazy-submission-pickle}).
With users excluded and submissions kept, every roster was gone from the file
and the same users were still in it, one on each submission.

So a stored submission leaves out the attributes that hold an object of an
excluded kind: a [[User]] when users are excluded, a [[Group]] when groups are.
We go by the value and not by the name, which also catches what a file from
before this filter already holds.
The submission in memory keeps what was attached, since the command is still
using it; we store a copy without those attributes.
Nothing is lost by it: the commands attach the student anew on every run.
Like the state filters, this applies only while the cache file is written.

What Canvas itself sends as part of a submission is another matter.
A submission names its student by identifier, and its comments and contents
may name them too; that is the submission, and leaving it out is what the kind
[[submission]] is for.
<<constants>>=
ATTACHED_KINDS = {"user": User, "group": canvasapi.group.Group}
<<functions>>=
def submission_for_cache_file(submission):
  """Return `submission` as the cache file may hold it.

  That is the submission itself, or while the cache file is written a copy
  without the attributes that hold an object of an excluded kind.
  """
  if not writing_to_cache_file.get():
    return submission
  excluded = tuple(cls for kind, cls in ATTACHED_KINDS.items()
                   if kind in CACHE_EXCLUDE)
  attached = [name for name, value in vars(submission).items()
              if isinstance(value, excluded)]
  if not attached:
    return submission
  stored = copy.copy(submission)
  for name in attached:
    delattr(stored, name)
  return stored
@

\paragraph{Misspelled kinds}
A user who excludes a kind relies on the data being left out, so a misspelled
kind must not go unnoticed.
[[CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE=users]] is an easy mistake: the kind is [[user]],
so nothing would be excluded.
Once all decorators are applied, we know every cached kind and can tell which
names of the setting are none of them.
<<functions>>=
def unknown_kinds(kinds):
  """Return the names in `kinds` that no decorator caches, sorted."""
  return sorted(set(kinds) - CACHED_KINDS)

def unknown_kinds_message(unknown):
  """Return what to tell the user about the `unknown` kinds in a setting."""
  return (f"ignoring unknown kind "
          f"{', '.join(repr(kind) for kind in unknown)}, so it is not left out "
          f"of the cache file; {known_kinds_text()}")

def known_kinds_text():
  """Return a sentence fragment listing the kinds that can be excluded."""
  return (f"the kinds are {', '.join(sorted(CACHED_KINDS))}, and personal "
          f"stands for {', '.join(sorted(PERSONAL_DATA_KINDS))}")
@

For the environment variable we find out at the end of the import, after the
[[make_*]] functions in the module outline have applied the decorators.
That is too early to log, for the reason \cref{sec:setting-problems} gives:
logging may not be configured yet, and a warning now could not be silenced
with [[-q]].
So the problem is recorded with the other malformed settings and reported with
them.
<<record the unknown kinds in the environment's cache exclusion>>=
if unknown_kinds(CACHE_EXCLUDE):
  SETTING_PROBLEMS["CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE"] = (
    "CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE: "
    + unknown_kinds_message(unknown_kinds(CACHE_EXCLUDE)))
