\subsection{Caching courses}

The list of courses changes whenever new courses are created that we have
access to.
This usually happens well in advance, but someone can also add us to a course
at any time, so the listing has a time-to-live of [[COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS]] days.

We use the [[CacheGetMethods]] decorator to add caching to the [[Canvas]] class
for its [[get_course]] and [[get_courses]] methods.
The decorator takes the attribute name [[course]] as argument.
A course Canvas no longer lists is forgotten if Canvas also answers 404 or 403
for it (\cref{sec:forget-unlisted}).
<<functions>>=
def make_canvas_courses_cacheable():
  import canvasapi.canvas
  canvasapi.canvas.Canvas = CacheGetMethods(
    "course", forget_unlisted=True, confirm_unlisted=True)(
      canvasapi.canvas.Canvas)
@

Note that we don't need a TTLCache here, since the [[outdated]] function will
periodically reset the [[_all_fetched]] flag to trigger a refetch.
One thing sets this listing apart from all the others: its owner, the [[Canvas]]
object, is in no cache, so the listing methods never call [[outdated]] on it.
The method checks the age of this listing itself instead
(\cref{sec:course-listing-outage}).


\subsection{Caching course contents}

Each course has assignments and users (students, teachers, TAs).
These change relatively infrequently:
Assignments are typically created at the beginning of the course and rarely
change after that.
Users are added at the beginning and occasionally during the course.

We use [[CacheGetMethods]] to cache both [[assignment]] and [[user]] attributes
of the [[Course]] class.
An assignment Canvas no longer lists is forgotten, while a user who has left is
kept, since their results still count (\cref{sec:forget-unlisted}).
<<functions>>=
def make_course_contents_cacheable():
  import canvasapi.course
  canvasapi.course.Course = CacheGetMethods("assignment", forget_unlisted=True)(
    canvasapi.course.Course)
  canvasapi.course.Course = CacheGetMethods("user")(
    canvasapi.course.Course)
@

\subsection{Caching pages}

Pages are wiki-style content in Canvas courses.
Unlike assignments and users which are identified by numeric IDs, pages are
identified by URL slugs (e.g., \enquote{course-syllabus} or \enquote{week-1-overview}).
This requires using the [[cache_key='url']] parameter to cache by URL instead
of ID.

The Canvas API provides both [[get_page(url)]] (singular) and [[get_pages()]]
(plural) methods, so we cache both.
<<functions>>=
def make_course_pages_cacheable():
  import canvasapi.course
  canvasapi.course.Course = CacheGetMethods("page", cache_key='url')(
    canvasapi.course.Course)
