        lazy.ensure_attrs(["grade", "missing_field"])

        assert lazy.missing_field is None
        assert lazy.grade == "F"
@

\paragraph{Performing the refresh}

Refreshing re-fetches the submission for the same user, assignment, and
[[include]] set.  We call the \emph{unwrapped} [[get_submission]] captured at
construction rather than the decorated one, so the refresh does not recurse back
through the cache and double-wrap the result.  Rebinding [[_submission]] updates
every holder of this wrapper at once, because they all share the one wrapper
object---which is exactly why the cache need not be touched separately.

Swapping the wrapped object has a side effect that is easy to miss.  The CLI
decorates submissions with attributes Canvas never sent: [[filter_submissions]]
attaches the matching [[User]] as [[submission.user]], and the listing helpers
attach the parent [[Assignment]] as [[submission.assignment]].  As we will see
under \enquote{Intercepting writes}, those annotations are stored on the
\emph{wrapped} submission---so a refresh, which replaces that object with a
freshly fetched one, silently drops them.  The symptom is an [[AttributeError]]
on [[submission.user.login_id]] in [[submissions view]], which reads the
mutable [[submitted_at]] (triggering the refresh) before it reads [[user]].
We therefore carry the recorded annotations over to the fresh submission.

The carry-over is deliberately one-sided: an annotation is re-applied only
when the fresh payload lacks the name.  Annotations are decorations Canvas
knows nothing about, so they can never conflict with the payload; but should
a caller ever write a payload attribute such as [[grade]] through the wrapper,
the value just fetched from Canvas is the truth and must win over the stale
local write.

The swap has a second casualty: the normalisation in the CLI's [[utils]]
module (\enquote{Normalizing partial Canvas objects}), which gives every
attribute the CLI reads a value, if only [[None]], so that it can read them
without [[getattr]] fallbacks.
Canvas leaves some fields out of its payload altogether---[[rubric_assessment]]
of a submission never assessed is one---so after a swap such a name is simply
gone, and a read that the listing helpers had guaranteed raises
[[AttributeError]] (issue~\#495).
The wrapper does not know the CLI's lists of attributes, but it does not need
to: a refresh must never \emph{shrink} the set of attributes, whatever put them
there.
So every name the old submission had and the fresh payload lacks becomes
[[None]] on the fresh submission.
We carry the name, not the old value: a field Canvas no longer reports has no
value any more, and the stale one would be a lie.
Annotations are applied first, so they keep their values; this only catches
what is left.

This does not happen because we asked for too little.  The wrapper refetches
with every [[include]] any caller has asked for so far (see below), and Canvas
omits the assessment of a never-assessed submission even when
[[include[]=rubric_assessment]] is given, from both the single-submission and
the list endpoint---we checked against the sandbox course.

Several code paths swap the wrapped submission: this lazy refresh, the bulk
refresh of a whole assignment (\cref{sec:bulk-refresh}), and a fetch of one
submission with an include it lacked.  All must keep the annotations and the
attribute names, so the swap is a method of its own rather than copies that
could drift apart.
<<LazySubmission methods>>=
def _replace_submission(self, fresh_submission):
  """Swap in `fresh_submission`, keeping what the old one had beyond it.

  Annotations are carried over unless Canvas sent that name; any other
  name the fresh payload lacks is kept as None.
  """
  submission = object.__getattribute__(self, '_submission')
  annotations = object.__getattribute__(self, '_annotations')
  for name, value in annotations.items():
    if not hasattr(fresh_submission, name):
      setattr(fresh_submission, name, value)
  for name in vars(submission):
    if not hasattr(fresh_submission, name):
      setattr(fresh_submission, name, None)
  object.__setattr__(self, '_submission', fresh_submission)
@

The lazy refresh fetches the submission anew and hands it over.

\subsubsection{When the submission is gone}
\label{sec:missing-submission}

A refetch can also fail outright.  On a finished course, students who have
since left it no longer have a submission to fetch: Canvas answers the
per-student submission request with a 404, which [[canvasapi]] raises as
[[ResourceDoesNotExist]].  Because the refresh is triggered from deep inside
an ordinary attribute read---[[submission.grade]] in [[results --missing]],
[[submission.submission_history]] in a grading criterion---an escaping
exception aborts the \emph{entire} command over a single departed student,
and the user gets a traceback instead of the results for everyone else.  One
student's gap must degrade into one student's stale row, not into total
failure, so we log a warning naming the submission we could not refresh and
go on serving the cached copy: it is the best answer we still have.

Marking the wrapper as refreshed although nothing was fetched looks wrong at
first glance, and it is a deliberate trade-off.  [[_needs_refresh]] is
consulted on \emph{every} read of a mutable attribute, so a wrapper left
stale would re-issue the doomed request for [[grade]], then again for
[[submission_history]], and so on for each such student---turning one missing
submission into a stream of 404s that slows the command to a crawl.
Advancing [[_last_refresh]] instead spends the failure once per TTL window.
The price is that a submission genuinely deleted on Canvas keeps its cached
value until the window expires, which is the cheaper of the two errors.

<<LazySubmission methods>>=
def _refresh(self):
  """Refresh the wrapped submission from Canvas.

  Caller annotations (attributes set through the wrapper, e.g. `user`)
  are carried over to the fresh submission unless Canvas sent that name;
  any other name the fresh payload lacks is kept as None.

  A submission Canvas no longer has (404) leaves the cached one in place;
  the wrapper still counts as refreshed, so the failing request is not
  repeated on every subsequent attribute access.
  """
  assignment = object.__getattribute__(self, '_assignment')
  submission = object.__getattribute__(self, '_submission')
  includes = object.__getattribute__(self, '_includes')
  original_get_submission = object.__getattribute__(self, '_original_get_submission')
  annotations = object.__getattribute__(self, '_annotations')

  # Call original method directly (bypasses decorator, no double-wrapping)
  try:
    fresh_submission = original_get_submission(
      assignment,
      submission.user_id,
      include=list(includes)
    )
  except canvasapi.exceptions.ResourceDoesNotExist:
    logger.warning(f"Could not refresh submission: user_id="
                   f"{submission.user_id} on assignment {assignment} "
                   f"no longer exists, keeping the cached submission")
  else:
    self._replace_submission(fresh_submission)

  object.__setattr__(self, '_last_refresh', datetime.now())
@

