\paragraph{Forcing a refresh before a write}

The staleness policy is tuned for \emph{reading}: serving a cached final grade
is harmless, because a final grade does not change.  A read-modify-write is a
different matter.  Setting one rubric criterion means sending the whole
assessment back to Canvas, since Canvas replaces the assessment rather than
merging into it (\cref{sec:rubric-merge}).  The unchanged criteria must
therefore come from what Canvas holds \emph{now}.  A cached assessment on a
submission graded [[P]] may be arbitrarily old---a colleague may have changed
it in SpeedGrader, or an earlier run of our own may have written it, which the
cache never saw because the edit response carries no assessment.  Writing that
back would silently revert those changes.

We therefore let a caller demand a refresh regardless of policy.  It is the
same refetch as the lazy one, so annotations survive it and a vanished
submission degrades to the cached copy in exactly the same way.
<<LazySubmission methods>>=
def refresh(self):
  """Refresh the wrapped submission from Canvas now, ignoring staleness.

  For callers about to write back data derived from the submission, where a
  cached final grade's attributes are not good enough.
  """
  object.__getattribute__(self, '_refresh')()
  return self
@

