\paragraph{Intercepting writes}

Writes mirror reads: internal state---the underscore-private names---stays on
the wrapper, while every other name is set on the wrapped submission so callers
can annotate it.  Setting the value on the wrapped submission alone is not
enough, though: as explained under \enquote{Performing the refresh}, the
wrapped object is replaced on refresh, so we also record every such write in
[[_annotations]] for [[_refresh]] to re-apply.  Recording happens only on this
path---[[ensure_attrs]] writes its [[None]] fill-ins directly to the wrapped
submission and is thus, by design, not an annotation; a refresh keeps those
names by other means, as [[None]] (\enquote{Performing the refresh}).

<<LazySubmission methods>>=
def __setattr__(self, name, value):
  """
  Intercept attribute setting to handle both wrapper and submission attrs.

  Internal attributes (_*) are set on the wrapper.
  All other attributes are set on the wrapped submission and remembered
  as annotations so that they survive a refresh.
  """
  if name.startswith('_'):
    object.__setattr__(self, name, value)
  else:
    object.__getattribute__(self, '_annotations')[name] = value
    submission = object.__getattribute__(self, '_submission')
    setattr(submission, name, value)
@

\paragraph{Writes that Canvas answers}

Not every write passes through [[__setattr__]].
[[submission.edit(...)]], with which [[submissions grade]] sets a grade, is a
method lookup: [[edit]] is not one of the wrapper's own members, so
[[__getattribute__]] delegates it, and the bound method merges Canvas's response
straight into the wrapped submission.
That is what we want.
The cached copy learns the new grade without a refetch, and since the merge
bypasses [[__setattr__]], it records no annotation that could later override a
fresh payload.
It also means that whatever [[edit]] leaves behind is what the cache saves
and---for a final grade, which is never refetched---what every later command
reads.
\Cref{SubmissionEditAttachments} makes [[edit]] leave proper [[File]]
attachments behind.
Let us verify that grading through the wrapper, as [[submissions grade]] does
on a cached submission, leaves [[File]] objects for a later
[[submissions view]] to read.
