\subsubsection{Pickle support for cache persistence}
\label{sec:lazy-submission-pickle}

The [[LazySubmission]] wrapper must support pickling to enable cache
persistence.
When the [[Canvas]] object is saved to disk (see cache.nw), Python's
[[pickle]] module serializes the entire object hierarchy, including all
cached submissions.

\paragraph{The pickling challenge}

Python's [[pickle]] cannot serialize certain objects, including:
\begin{itemize}
\item Function and method references (unless they're module-level functions)
\item Lambda functions
\item Generator objects
\item Objects with circular references to unpicklable objects
\end{itemize}

Our [[LazySubmission]] stores [[_original_get_submission]], which is
a reference to the original (unwrapped) [[get_submission]] method.
This is a \emph{bound method} when called, but we store the \emph{unbound
function} from the decorator's closure.
Method objects are not picklable by default.

\paragraph{The solution: find it again on the class}

We implement [[__getstate__]] and [[__setstate__]] to handle
pickling:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Before pickling} ([[__getstate__]]): Exclude the
  unpicklable [[_original_get_submission]] from the state dictionary.
  Save only the submission, assignment reference, includes list, and last
  refresh timestamp.  The annotation record is left out too, but for a
  different reason: annotations are per-run decorations that the CLI
  re-applies on every invocation (for instance [[filter_submissions]] sets
  [[user]] anew each run), so persisting the record would only freeze one
  run's view of the data.  The annotated attributes themselves are on the
  wrapped submission and are stored with it, except those that hold an
  object of a kind the user keeps out of the cache file
  (\cref{sec:cache-exclude}).
\item \textbf{After unpickling} ([[__setstate__]]): Start with an empty
  annotation record and take [[_original_get_submission]] from the class of
  the assignment, where the decorator keeps it as
  [[uncached_get_submission]] (\cref{sec:caching-submissions}).
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Why the class, and not the closure}

The original method is the same for every submission of every assignment, so
it does not belong in each wrapper's stored state; it belongs to the class,
and a class is not pickled, only named.
The decorator that wraps [[Assignment.get_submission]] therefore leaves the
method it wrapped on the class, under a name of its own.

An earlier version had no such attribute and searched for the method instead:
the decorated method keeps the original in its [[__closure__]], and
[[__setstate__]] took the first callable there with [[get_submission]] in its
name.
That depended on how the decorator happened to be written, down to the names
of its variables, and when the search found nothing it left the attribute
unset, so the first refresh failed with an [[AttributeError]] far from its
cause (issue~\#317).
An attribute lookup depends on one name that both sides state.

\paragraph{Limitations and failure modes}

Only one remains.
If the class of the assignment has no [[uncached_get_submission]], we set
[[_original_get_submission]] to [[None]]: the wrapper can still be read, since
attributes pass through to the wrapped submission, but it cannot refresh.
For a real [[Assignment]] that cannot happen, because unpickling a
[[LazySubmission]] imports this module, and importing it decorates the class.
It is the case of a stand-in for an assignment, as in some tests.

\paragraph{Alternative approaches considered}

We considered but rejected these alternatives:
\begin{description}
\item[Storing method name as string] Reconstruct via
  [[getattr(assignment, 'get_submission')]].
  Problem: This retrieves the \emph{decorated} method, not the original,
  causing infinite recursion or double-wrapping.
\item[Not using a wrapper] Store a \enquote{needs refresh} flag on the
  submission itself.
  Problem: Mutating Canvas API objects is fragile and breaks the lazy-loading
  design.
\item[Store assignment ID, re-fetch assignment] Reconstruct the entire context
  from IDs.
  Problem: Defeats the purpose of caching; would require API calls just to
  restore cache state.
\end{description}

Now we implement the pickle support:
<<pickle support for LazySubmission>>=
def __getstate__(self):
  """
  Prepare object for pickling.

  We can't pickle method references, so we exclude _original_get_submission.
  It is taken from the assignment's class again during unpickling.
  """
  state = {
    '_submission': submission_for_cache_file(
      object.__getattribute__(self, '_submission')),
    '_assignment': object.__getattribute__(self, '_assignment'),
    '_includes': object.__getattribute__(self, '_includes'),
    '_last_refresh': object.__getattribute__(self, '_last_refresh'),
  }
  return state

def __setstate__(self, state):
  """
  Restore object after unpickling.

  Restore all attributes from state, and take _original_get_submission from
  the class of the assignment, where the caching decorator keeps it.
  """
  for key, value in state.items():
    object.__setattr__(self, key, value)
  object.__setattr__(self, '_annotations', {})
  object.__setattr__(
    self, '_original_get_submission',
    getattr(type(state['_assignment']), 'uncached_get_submission', None))
@

