\subsection{Caching files and folders}
\label{sec:caching-files}

A course's files are what its pages and assignments link to, and a link names
a file by its path: the folder it is in and its name.
Translating links (see [[cli/links.nw]]) therefore asks for files and folders
all the time, a file by its identifier when a page is exported and the files
of a folder when it is imported again, and the [[files]] command lists them
all.
None of this was cached, so each run paid for it again: two requests for every
distinct file a page links to (issue~\#477).

We cache both on the [[Course]], like its other contents.
<<functions>>=
def make_course_files_cacheable():
  """Add caching to Course.get_file/get_files and get_folder/get_folders"""
  import canvasapi.course
  canvasapi.course.Course = CacheGetMethods("file", forget_unlisted=True)(
    canvasapi.course.Course)
  canvasapi.course.Course = CacheGetMethods("folder", forget_unlisted=True)(
    canvasapi.course.Course)
@

Both listings forget what Canvas no longer returns
(\cref{sec:forget-unlisted}).
A file that is replaced gets a new identifier, so without forgetting, the
listing would show the old file next to the new one for good, and a deleted
file would never go away.
Nothing is cached below a file or a folder, so nothing else is lost with it.

No new schema version is needed for the two new caches, since the wrapped
methods create the attributes of a course that was stored without them
(\cref{sec:cache-attributes-on-read}).

\subsubsection{Files that are only ever fetched one by one}
\label{sec:files-fetched-alone}

The listings expire like any other, after [[DEFAULT_CACHE_TTL_DAYS]] days, and
the fetch that follows replaces every entry.
But exporting a page never reads a listing.
It asks for one file at a time, by identifier, and an object fetched that way
does not expire: [[outdated]] has no rule for it.
For most kinds that is harmless, because their listings are read often enough
to replace the entries.
A file that has since been renamed or moved in Canvas would keep its old path
in every exported link until someone asked for [[--refresh-cache]].

So when one file or folder is asked for and the cached one is older than a
listing is allowed to be, we fetch it again.
The rule belongs to the singular method and not to [[outdated]], which the
plural method also asks about every entry.
There it would do harm: a listing can stay in use for a day after its entries
have passed the limit, since its timestamp is only reset when [[outdated]]
looks at the course, and then every entry would be fetched on its own, one
request for each file of the course where one listing would do.
A listing has its own time to live, and that is the one that should replace
its entries.
<<functions>>=
def fetched_alone_long_ago(obj):
  """Return True if `obj` is a file or folder older than a listing may be."""
  fetched_at = getattr(obj, "_fetched_at", None)
  return (isinstance(obj, (canvasapi.file.File, canvasapi.folder.Folder))
          and fetched_at is not None
          and datetime.now() - fetched_at
              > timedelta(days=DEFAULT_CACHE_TTL_DAYS))
@

Let's verify this through the real [[Course]], with [[responses]] in place of
