\subsection{Caching submissions}
\label{sec:caching-submissions}

For the results, we can construct something more specific.
Results that we're interested in are submissions.
We can only get submission from an assignment object.
We can either get all submissions, or one specific submission if we specify the
user.

The cache holds an assignment's complete collection of submissions, and only
that.
Canvas's [[get_submissions()]] also accepts arguments that filter submissions
server-side, such as [[bucket="ungraded"]], but we keep no cache per filter.
Separate caches for different [[bucket]] values would hold the same
submissions more than once, each refreshed on its own, so they could disagree,
and the cache hierarchy (course caches contain assignment caches contain
submission caches) would no longer have one place for each submission.
Instead, client code (particularly [[submissions.nw]] and [[assignments.nw]])
fetches the complete collection and filters it locally, see
\cref{SubmissionsFiltering} in [[submissions.nw]].
A library caller who passes such an argument gets what it asked for, but
straight from Canvas and uncached, as \cref{sec:submission-arguments} explains.

Now we combine the caching mechanism with lazy loading.
The cache stores [[LazySubmission]] wrappers instead of raw submissions.
This means submissions are only refreshed when their attributes are actually
accessed, not when the list is returned.
<<functions>>=
