\subsubsection{Submissions Canvas no longer lists}
\label{sec:unlisted-submissions}

The timestamps above settle when a submission is fresh, but not what it holds.
A fetch only replaces the submissions Canvas returns, so the submission of a
student who has left the course keeps the includes of the last fetch that returned
it.
If a listing is then asked for an include it lacks, a fetch can't give it that
while Canvas doesn't list it, and if the listing compared with it like any other,
every such read would fetch the whole assignment again.
A student can also return, though, and then only a fetch can tell us.

So [[__unlisted]] holds the users whose submissions the last fetch of the
collection did not return, and in [[<<return a list of all submissions>>]] those
only count once that fetch is old enough, by the rule for a name that matches
nothing (\cref{sec:refresh-on-miss}).
Until then a student who has returned is served without that include, and a
student who has left for good costs one fetch per interval, for reads asking for
what they lack, on the same terms as a listing (\cref{sec:subset-listings}).
We keep this on the assignment, beside [[__all_fetched]], since it is a fact about
that fetch and not about the submission, and it follows the assignment when a
listing replaces the object, as the caches do.
It's also why we don't simply keep the includes of the last fetch there and
compare with those.
A submission fetched on its own, say of a student who just joined, can hold less
than the listing asked for, and must still make the next listing fetch it.

We also can't record the new includes on these wrappers.
[[_includes]] says what the wrapper really holds, and a request for that one
submission relies on it to see what it must fetch.
What these submissions do hold still counts towards the largest set, since
[[need_refetch]] takes the union over the whole cache.

An assignment from a cache written by an earlier version has no [[__unlisted]].
It behaves as if all submissions were listed, costs one more fetch and then has
it.

\subsubsection{When the bulk refresh fails}
\label{sec:bulk-refresh-failure}

That refetch asks Canvas for an entire assignment's submissions, and such a
request can fail like any other: a 404 for an assignment removed while the
report was running, a 500 from an overloaded Canvas.  Uncaught, the exception
travels out of [[get_submissions]], through the listing generators in
[[submissions.nw]], and aborts the whole [[results]] or [[submissions]]
command---although we are holding, right here, a complete copy of every
submission that is at worst a few minutes stale.  This is the failure class
\cref{sec:missing-submission} fixed one level down for the per-submission
refresh, and it deserves the same answer: a failed request should cost the
report one assignment's freshness, not the report itself.  So we log which
assignment we could not refresh and keep serving the cache.

The two guards differ in \emph{which} exceptions they excuse, deliberately so.
[[_refresh]] catches only [[ResourceDoesNotExist]], because it knows precisely
which failure it is excusing---the one student who has left the course---and a
500 on a single submission is a surprise worth a traceback.  The bulk refetch
has no such precision: it stands between the user and \emph{every} row of the
report, so whatever it lets escape takes all the other assignments and students
with it.  Its scope is collateral where [[_refresh]]'s is targeted, and we
therefore catch [[CanvasException]], the base class of everything [[canvasapi]]
raises.

The objection to so broad a catch is that it also swallows systemic failures,
an expired token say, which the user would rather see at once.  It does not,
and the reason is structural: only the \emph{re}fresh of data we already have
is guarded.  The fetch of an assignment's submissions in the [[need_refetch]]
branch above is left unguarded.  The first time, there is no cached answer to
fall back on there, so a dead server or a rejected token still fails loudly on
the first assignment the command touches, long before this code could hide it.
Under [[--refresh-cache]] there is a cached answer, but the user has just told us
not to trust it, so reporting the failure is again the honest thing to do.

Logging and moving on is not enough on its own, because of how the code finds
its way back here.  [[should_bulk_refresh_submissions]] asks the wrappers
whether they would refresh, and the listing commands call [[get_submissions]]
several times per assignment in a single run.  Leave the wrappers untouched and
the cache answers that question the same way on the next call, re-issuing the
same doomed request; and in between, every read of a mutable attribute falls
back to its own per-submission refresh.  One failed bulk fetch would thus
produce exactly the storm the bulk path exists to prevent---the 40 calls we
went to this trouble to turn into 1.

We therefore mark every cached wrapper as refreshed as of now, just as
[[_refresh]] advances [[_last_refresh]] on the single submission it could not
fetch (\cref{sec:missing-submission}).  The resulting invariant is easy to
state: once this code has run, in success or in failure, every cached wrapper
counts as fresh for one TTL window.  Marking \emph{all} of them, rather than
only those in [[student_ids]], mirrors what a successful fetch does---it
replaces the whole collection---and it is what makes the invariant hold for the
next caller, who may well arrive with a different scope.  If the failure struck
partway through a paginated response, the submissions already replaced simply
have their timestamp set to now a second time.

The price is [[_refresh]]'s price one notch wider: data Canvas would have
handed us is served stale until the window expires.  Against a report that does
not run at all, that is the cheaper error.

We leave [[__all_fetched]] alone.  It records when the collection was last
genuinely fetched---for the cache-age log line above, and through its [[None]]
value for the question of whether any fetch has happened at all---so letting a
failed refetch touch it would only make that log lie.  It is [[_last_refresh]]
on the wrappers, not [[__all_fetched]], that gates the retry.

<<keep the cached submissions after a failed bulk refresh>>=
logger.warning(f"Could not refresh all submissions for assignment "
               f"id={self.id}: {error}; keeping the "
               f"{len(self.__cache)} cached submissions")

for lazy_submission in self.__cache.values():
  object.__setattr__(lazy_submission, '_last_refresh', datetime.now())
