\subsubsection{What a caller asks for}
\label{sec:submission-arguments}

The two getters above read their keyword arguments themselves instead of
handing them on to [[canvasapi]], and so they must read them the way
[[canvasapi]] would, since that is what Canvas would have received.

Canvas accepts [[include]] as a single value as well as a list, and so does
[[canvasapi]], which makes [[include="user"]] a correct call, as is a tuple or a
set of includes.
Turned into a set as it stands, the string becomes the set of its letters,
[[{"u", "s", "e", "r"}]], so the caller gets no user and no error.
Worse, the letters become the submission's [[_includes]], the record of what
the cache holds, so they are sent again with every later fetch of the
assignment.
We therefore read the value through [[as_list]], as [[must_update]] does.
It takes a value apart the way [[canvasapi]] does, so a string counts as a list
of one and any other iterable as its items.
An [[include=None]] asks for nothing, as it does in [[canvasapi]], whose
request leaves out a parameter whose value is [[None]].
Read through [[as_list]] alone it would be the include [[None]], recorded in
[[_includes]] as if the submission held it, so we read it as no list at all.
<<let [[to_include]] be the includes asked for>>=
to_include = set(as_list(kwargs.get("include") or []))
@

Let's verify both getters through the real wrapper on a real [[Assignment]].
What we must see is the query each one sends, so [[responses]] stands in for
Canvas at the HTTP layer and records it; everything above that layer runs as
in production.
We collect the tests in the [[<<submission arguments test methods>>]] bucket
chunk.
