A caller can pass the listing other arguments too, and some of them ask for
part of the collection, [[bucket="ungraded"]] for instance, which is meant to
leave out what is graded, or ask for it in another shape, as [[grouped]] does
for group assignments.
The cache cannot answer such a call.
It holds the complete collection, and the answer to such a call is not one.
Sending only the includes, as the listing used to, gave a caller who asked for
the ungraded submissions every submission, with no sign that [[bucket]] was
ignored.
Storing the answer would be worse.
For submissions the cache records only the includes, so nothing would mark the
answer as a part, and every later listing would be served the ungraded
submissions as if they were all of them.
That holds for the older copies of this library that share the cache file
([[nytid]], for instance) as much as for us.
It is the problem \cref{sec:subset-listings} describes for a course's users.

So the submission getters follow the rule [[CacheGetMethods]] follows, and use
its test, [[restricts_listing]]: a call with any argument other than
[[include]] and the keys for sorting and page size goes straight to Canvas, with
all its arguments, and leaves the cache alone.
The answer is [[canvasapi]]'s own submissions, not [[LazySubmission]] wrappers,
since there is no cache entry for them to refresh.
We return them as a list, as the cached path does, so that the caller can, for
instance, take its length whichever path answered.
<<pass a request for some submissions through to Canvas>>=
if restricts_listing(kwargs):
  logger.info(f"Not cached: submissions of assignment id={self.id} "
              f"restricted by {kwargs}")
  return list(get_submissions(self, *args, **kwargs))
@

The listing has two keyword arguments of its own, [[check_bulk_refresh]] and
[[student_ids]], which are not Canvas's.
The listing takes them out of [[kwargs]] before this test, so they never count
as a restriction and are never sent to Canvas.
Canvas's endpoint for the submissions of several assignments has a
[[student_ids]] parameter too, but this one is ours: it only scopes the decision
on a bulk refresh to the users a filtered listing will read.

Canvas documents only [[include]] for a single submission, but the rule is the
same: whatever else a caller passes reaches Canvas, and the answer does not
replace the cached submission.
<<pass a restricted request for one submission through to Canvas>>=
if restricts_listing(kwargs):
  logger.info(f"Not cached: submission user_id={uid} restricted by {kwargs}")
  return get_submission(self, user, **kwargs)
@

None of canvaslms's own commands passes such an argument.
They fetch the complete collection and filter it locally
(\cref{SubmissionsFiltering}), so they keep what the cache saves them.

Let's verify that a restricted call reaches Canvas with all its arguments,
returns what Canvas returned and leaves the cache as it was: empty, so that the
next plain listing fetches the whole collection, or full, so that it is still
served from the cache.
<<submission arguments test methods>>=
@responses.activate
def test_a_restricted_listing_is_sent_whole_and_not_cached(self):
    assignment, sent = self.assignment()

    ungraded = assignment.get_submissions(bucket="ungraded", include=["user"])

    assert sent[0]["bucket"] == ["ungraded"]
    assert sent[0]["include[]"] == ["user"]
    assert [each.id for each in ungraded] == [3]
    assert getattr(assignment, "__cache") == {}
    assert getattr(assignment, "__all_fetched") is None

    assignment.get_submissions()

    assert len(sent) == 2
    assert "bucket" not in sent[1]

